\section{Gravity potential}
The gravity potential $W=\Phi+V$ of a celestial body is the sum of the centrifugal potential $\Phi$ from rotation and gravitational potential $V=V_{\rm static}+\delta V$, which includes a dominant static part $V_{\rm static}$ and a time-varying component $\delta V$. For a point with radial distance $r$, polar distance $\theta$, and longitude $\lambda$, $V_{\rm static}$ is calculated from the normalized Stokes coefficients $\bar{C}_{nm}$ and $\bar{S}_{nm}$ (degree $n$ and order $m$) based on a static gravity field model and the normalized associated Legendre function $\bar{P}_{nm}(\cos\theta)$:
\begin{multline}
    V_{\rm static} = \frac{GM}{r} 
    \Bigg[ 1 + \sum_{n=2}^\infty \left( \frac{a}{r} \right)^n 
    \sum_{m=0}^n \bar{P}_{nm}(\cos\theta) \\
    \times \big( \bar{C}_{nm} \cos m\lambda + \bar{S}_{nm} \sin m\lambda \big) \Bigg],
\label{eq5}
\end{multline}
where $G$, $M$, and $a$ represent the gravitational constant, the body's mass and the equatorial radius, respectively \cite{HofmannWellenhof2006}. The time-varying component, calculated as an effective potential variation, $\delta V=\sum_i\left(1+k_i+h_i\right)\delta V_i$ is a sum of harmonics. $\delta V_i$ represents the direct potential effects of various tidal and non-tidal geophysical forcings, e.g., tidal forces, the attraction of the moving ocean water or air masses, the centrifugal effect of polar motion and hydrological signals. The (load) Love numbers $k_i$ and $h_i$, varying with degree $n$ and order $m$, characterize the body's response to these forcings, including the induced gravitational potential change and surface deformations \cite{Farrell1972,Love1911,Voigt2016,Schroeder2021,Vincent2024a}. In our simulation, we consider both tidal and non-tidal variations in $\delta V$ for the Earth, and only tidal effects for the Moon. The centrifugal potential $ \Phi $ is determined by the body’s angular velocity $\bm{\omega}$ and the clock’s position $\bm{r}$ on the body surface:
\begin{equation}
\Phi = \frac{1}{2} (\bm{\omega} \times \bm{r})^2\;. 
\label{eq6}
\end{equation} 


\subsection{E-clocks}
Considering the Moon’s orbital period, the daily Earth gravity field model ITSG-Grace2018 is used to represent both static and non-tidal temporal potential (hydrological, cryospheric, and glacial isostatic adjustment signals) \cite{MayerGuerr2018, Kvas2019}, denoted as $V_{\rm ITSG}$. The AOD1B RL06 time series \cite{Shihora2022} is used to model the potential variation $V_{\rm AOD}$ caused by non-tidal atmospheric and oceanic mass variability. To fully evaluate effective potential variations from the non-tidal part, surface deformation effects $V_{\rm GNSSh}=\sum_i h_i \delta V_i$ are calculated using daily GNSS displacement $\delta H$ \cite{Blewitt2018} from selected stations (MAUI, WTZR, GRAS \cite{RESIF2017}, MDO1, P027 \cite{Community2007}, MATE) and gravity acceleration $g$, simplified as $\sum_i h_i \delta V_i = g\delta H$ \cite{Schroeder2021, Vincent2024a}. Effective potential variations of solid Earth tides $V_{\rm EP-SET}$ and pole tides $V_{\rm EP-SEP}$ are computed with the modified tool ETERNA 3.4 \cite{Wenzel1996}, and ocean tide loading $V_{\rm EP-OTL}$ with SPOTL 3.3.0.2 \cite{Agnew2012, Agnew1997}. Other tidal factors, like ocean pole and atmospheric tides, are ignored. Earth’s angular velocity for the centrifugal potential $\Phi_E$ is derived from IERS EOP C04 LOD data \cite{Bizouard2019} with corrections from ocean tides and libration \cite{Petit2010}.

The contributions of individual components of the E-clock gravity potential $W_{E}$—$\Phi_E$, $V_{\rm ITSG}$, $V_{\rm AOD}$, $V_{\rm GNSSh}$, $V_{\rm EP-SET}$, $V_{\rm EP-OTL}$, $V_{\rm EP-SEP}$—to E–L clock observations are evaluated as their values divided by $c^2$. Their approximate values in \hyperref[Tab1]{Tab.~\ref{Tab1}} are calculated using the full model series of LOD (1962-2025), ITSG (2002–2017), AOD (1976–2025) and GNSS (station-dependent) with the latter three restricted to ITSG’s timespan (2002–2017). The dominant terms are $V_{\rm ITSG}$ and $\Phi_E$, about $6.96 \times 10^{-10}$ and from $5.14 \times 10^{-13}$ to $10.49 \times 10^{-13}$ for the six stations, while the smallest terms $V_{\rm EP-SEP}$ and $V_{\rm AOD}$ are around $1 \times 10^{-18}$. Based on the available uncertainties of ITSG and GNSS models, $V_{\rm ITSG}/c^2$ and $V_{\rm GNSSh}/c^2$ uncertainties at all six clock locations are calculated over the full model timespans. Except for a few epochs (outside 2009) of the E-clocks at OCA and MLRO for the GNSS model slightly exceeding $1 \times 10^{-18}$, all values remain within this limit, including their combined uncertainty.

