The lunar tidal potential $V_{\rm LTP}$ at a position $p_m$ on the Moon with spherical coordinates $(r_{p_m}, \theta_{p_m}, \lambda_{p_m})$ resulting from a tide-generating body $b$ with mass $M_b$ and coordinates $(r_b, \theta_b, \lambda_b)$ at time $t$ can be calculated by \cite{Wenzel1997}
\begin{multline}
    V_{\rm LTP} = \frac{GM_b}{r_b} \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \left( \frac{r_{p_m}}{r_b} \right)^n \frac{1}{2n + 1} \sum_{m=0}^{n} \bar{P}_{nm}(\cos \theta_{p_m}) \\
   \times \bar{P}_{nm}(\cos \theta_b) 
    \cdot \cos \big( m\lambda_{p_m} - m\lambda_b \big).
\label{eq7}
\end{multline}
The bodies’ ephemerides are generated by numerically integrating a DE440-based dynamical model \cite{Park2021} in the LLR software LUNAR \cite{Zhang2023, Singh2023, Mueller2019, Hofmann2017, Biskupek2015} with lunar initial states and other parameters fitted to over 53 years of LLR data. Given the clock uncertainty of $10^{-18}$, only Earth and Sun contributions up to degrees $n=3$ and 2 are sufficient; higher degrees and other bodies are negligible. For the lunar effective tidal potential $V_{\rm ELTP}$, degree-2 Love numbers ($k_{l2}$ and $h_{l2}$) and the associated tidal time delay are considered.

The angular velocity of the lunar mantle $\bm{\omega}_L$ is integrated concurrently with the ephemerides, based on its time derivative \citep[Eq. (34)]{Folkner2014}, to calculate the lunar centrifugal potential $\Phi_L$. 

\hyperref[Tab3]{Tab.~\ref{Tab3}} summarizes the contributions of $W_L$ elements to $\Delta f/f_L$: $V_{\rm GRAIL}/c^2$ about $3.14 \times 10^{-11}$, $\Phi_L/c^2$ around $1 \times 10^{-16}$, and $V_{\rm ELTP}/c^2$ ranging from $-0.3 \times 10^{-16}$ to $2.6 \times 10^{-16} $ for the five reflectors. Variations in $V_{\rm ELTP}/c^2$ and $\Phi_L/c^2$ are derived from over 50 years of ephemerides and a $\bm{\omega}_L$ series. In $V_{\rm ELTP}/c^2$, the dominant contribution comes from Earth (up to degree 3), while effects from the Sun (degree 2) and lunar response ($k_{l2}$ and $h_{l2}$ part) are smaller, varying from $-0.7 \times 10^{-18}$ to $1.4 \times 10^{-18}$ and $-5.0 \times 10^{-18}$ to $0.6 \times 10^{-18}$, respectively. Earth degree 3 alone contributes $-0.6 \times 10^{-18}$ to $1.2 \times 10^{-18}$. 

