\section{Earth-Moon coordinate time ratio}
We analyze the Earth-Moon time ratio $F/c^2$ in \hyperref[eq4]{Eq.~\eqref{eq4}} using two approaches. 
First, the relationships between Barycentric Coordinate Time ($t\equiv$ TCB) and Terrestrial Coordinate Time ($u\equiv$ TCG), and between TCB and Lunar Coordinate Time ($s\equiv$ TCL) follow \citep[Eqs. (1) and (2)]{Kopeikin2024}. At the L-clock's location, $F/c^2=(ds_L/dt)(dt/du_L)-1$. By computing the derivatives, we obtain:
\begin{multline}
    \frac{F}{c^2} = 
    -\frac{1}{c^2} \Bigl[ 
     \sum_{A \neq L} \frac{GM_A}{r_{LA}} 
    - \sum_{A \neq E} \frac{GM_A}{r_{EA}}+\frac{v_L^2 - v_E^2}{2} \\
    +  \bm{a}_L \cdot \bm{r}_{pL} 
    + \bm{v}_L \cdot \bm{v}_{pL} 
    - \bm{a}_E \cdot \bm{r}_{pE} 
    - \bm{v}_E \cdot \bm{v}_{pE}  
    \Bigr]+O(c^{-4})\;. 
\label{eq8}
\end{multline}
We use BCRS coordinates ${\bm x}_E$, ${\bm x}_L$, ${\bm x}_p$ of the Earth, Moon and L-clock, and their velocities ${\bm v}_E$, ${\bm v}_L$, ${\bm v}_p$, respectively. We denote ${\bm r}_{pE} = {\bm x}_p - {\bm x}_E$, ${\bm r}_{pL} = {\bm x}_p - {\bm x}_L$ and $\bm{v}_{pE} = \bm{v}_p - \bm{v}_E$, $\bm{v}_{pL} = \bm{v}_p - \bm{v}_L$. The BCRS accelerations of the Earth and Moon are $\bm{a}_E$ and $\bm{a}_L$. The distances $r_{EA}$ and $r_{LA}$ are between body $A$ with mass $M_A$ and Earth, and between body $A$ and Moon. 
Second, we use a theoretical relation between TCG and TCL derived by \citet[Eq. (12)]{Kopeikin2024}, which does not rely on using the BCRS as an intermediate coordinate system. By computing the derivative from this relation and taking into account terms of the second and third order of the tidal potential $U$, we obtain:    
\begin{multline}
\frac{F}{c^2} = -\frac{1}{c^2}\Bigl(\bm{a}_{LE} \cdot \bm{r}_{pL} + \bm{v}_{LE} \cdot \bm{v}_{pL}+\frac{v_{LE}^2}{2} \\
+ \frac{G M_E}{r_{LE}} - \frac{2G M_L}{r_{LE}}+U_2+U_3\Bigr)+O(c^{-4})\;,
\label{eq9}
\end{multline}
where ${\bm r}_{LE} = {\bm x}_L - {\bm x}_E$, ${\bm v}_{LE} = {\bm v}_L - {\bm v}_E$ and ${\bm a}_{LE} = {\bm a}_L - {\bm a}_E$, and 
\begin{equation}
U_2=\sum\limits_{A \neq E, L}\frac{G M_A}{2r_{EA}}\left(\frac{r_{LE}}{r_{EA}}\right)^2\left(3\cos^2\psi_{LA} - 1  \right)\;,
\label{eq10}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
U_3=\sum\limits_{A \neq E, L}\frac{G M_A}{2r_{EA}}\left(\frac{r_{LE}}{r_{EA}}\right)^3\left(5\cos^3\psi_{LA}-3\cos\psi_{LA}\right)\;.
\label{eq11}
\end{equation}
Here, $\cos\psi_{LA}={\bm n}_{AE} \cdot {\bm n}_{LE}$, where $\psi_{LA}$ is the angle between the two unit vectors ${\bm n}_{AE}$ and ${\bm n}_{LE}$, which are directed from Earth's geocenter to body $A$ and the Moon, respectively. 

Barycentric Dynamical Time (TDB) is used in LUNAR for calculating the ephemerides, while the quantities in \hyperref[eq8]{Eqs.~(\ref{eq8})} and \hyperref[eq9]{(\ref{eq9})} are TCB-compatible. TDB is scaled to TCB by the factor $(1-L_B)$, where $L_B=1.550519768 \times 10^{-8}$ \cite{Petit2010} along with corresponding re-scaling of spatial coordinates and body masses. This procedure leaves \hyperref[eq8]{Eq.~(\ref{eq8})} and \hyperref[eq9]{Eq.~(\ref{eq9})} unchanged \cite{Klioner2008}. 

Due to mutual interactions among bodies, the accelerations, velocities, and positions in \hyperref[eq8]{Eqs.~(\ref{eq8})} and \hyperref[eq9]{(\ref{eq9})} inherently reflect combined effects. As a result, the isolated contribution of a single body cannot be uniquely extracted. We therefore evaluate the effect of a body only based on the terms in which it appears directly in the equations. For Earth and Moon, these include the acceleration-, velocity-, and gravitational potential-related terms in both \hyperref[eq8]{Eqs.~(\ref{eq8})} and \hyperref[eq9]{(\ref{eq9})}, but the two equations provide different expressions for them. For other bodies, only the potential terms in \hyperref[eq8]{Eq.~(\ref{eq8})}, and the tidal terms in \hyperref[eq9]{Eq.~(\ref{eq9})} are involved.

\hyperref[Tab4]{Tab.~\ref{Tab4}} presents the effects on $F/c^2$ in \hyperref[eq8]{Eqs.~(\ref{eq8})} and \hyperref[eq9]{(\ref{eq9})} from the ten major solar-system bodies: Earth, Moon, Sun, and the other seven planets. The effect of each body differs between the two equations due to the differences in the terms used. However, in both cases, the largest contributions to $F/c^2$ come from the Earth, Moon, and Sun, followed by Jupiter (the most massive solar-system planet) and Venus (with the orbit closest to the Earth's); the other planets contribute less. Compared to \hyperref[eq8]{Eq.~(\ref{eq8})}, Venus contributes more than Jupiter in \hyperref[eq9]{Eq.~(\ref{eq9})}, as the distance weights more than mass in tidal potentials. The same holds for Mars and Saturn. 

Despite the differences in individual contributions, the total effect from all bodies remains consistent between the two equations. For the $10^{-18}$ clock uncertainty, \hyperref[eq8]{Eq.~(\ref{eq8})} requires all ten major solar-system bodies. In contrast, \hyperref[eq9]{Eq.~(\ref{eq9})} matches \hyperref[eq8]{Eq.~(\ref{eq8})}, at the $10^{-18}$ level using only Earth, Moon, and Sun, with occasional differences exceeding $1 \times 10^{-17}$ (\hyperref[Fig3]{Fig.~\ref{Fig3}(a)}). Adding Venus and Jupiter further decreases the overall differences (\hyperref[Fig3]{Fig.~\ref{Fig3}(b)}). While both equations identify the Earth, Moon, and Sun as dominant contributors, \hyperref[eq9]{Eq.~(\ref{eq9})} places even greater emphasis on them. The $F/c^2$ differences among L-clock sites reach the $10^{-15}$ level, computed using either \hyperref[eq8]{Eq.~(\ref{eq8})} or \hyperref[eq9]{Eq.~(\ref{eq9})} . 

