Based on \hyperref[Tab1]{Tabs.~\ref{Tab1}} and \hyperref[Tab3]{\ref{Tab3}}, the effects on $\Delta f/f_L$ from the gravity potentials of Earth clocks $W_E/c^2$ and Moon clocks $W_L/c^2$ are on the order of $10^{-10}$ and $10^{-11}$, respectively. Their differences $(W_E-W_L)/c^2$ are at the $10^{-10}$ level (see \hyperref[Fig5]{Fig.~\ref{Fig5}(a)} for the E-clock at APOLLO and L-clock at A15 in 2009). Results for $F/c^2$ from \hyperref[eq8]{Eqs.~(\ref{eq8})} and \hyperref[eq9]{(\ref{eq9})} are highly consistent, both indicating values at the $10^{-11}$ level. \hyperref[Fig5]{Fig.~\ref{Fig5}(b)} shows the result of \hyperref[eq8]{Eq.~(\ref{eq8})} for the L-clock at A15 in 2009, using the reception time series based on the signal from the E-clock at APOLLO. \hyperref[Fig5]{Fig.~\ref{Fig5}(c)} shows the simulated fractional frequency differences in 2009 for the E- and L-clocks assumed at APOLLO and A15.

