\section{Results}\label{sec:results}
\pageenlarge 1
Tables~\ref{tab:tab1} and~\ref{tab:tab2} present the performance of our three IPSsim
variants, IPSsim\textsubscript{(cosa)}, IPSsim\textsubscript{(euca)}, and IPSsim\textsubscript{(man)}, for \(T=5\) and \(T=10\). We compare IPSsim with the logging policy (LP), Naive, IPS, RPS, DR, MIPS\textsubscript{LTR}, and DRM approaches. Both tables show results under \(10^4\), \(10^6\), and \(10^8\) clicks for top-n settings where n\( \in \{10,30,50\}\). We also report the learned \(\alpha\) for each IPSsim variant.


From Table~\ref{tab:tab1} (ISTELLA-S), for n = 30 with \(10^4\), \(10^6\), and \(10^8\)
clicks, at least one IPSsim variant outperforms all other estimators; at n = 50, IPSsim
surpasses most baselines (including DR). From Table~\ref{tab:tab2} (MSLTR-WEB30K), for
n = 50 under \(10^6\) and \(10^8\) clicks, IPSsim again outperforms most baselines. Hence, we can answer \textbf{RQ1} positively: integrating document similarity into IPS improves
ranking effectiveness when n \(\ge 30\).

