It also utilizes \textit{Opaque Predicates} and \textit{Dead Code}. These are conditions and code blocks that do not affect the overall program logic but are intended to confuse the analyst. \textit{Opaque predicates} are conditions that always evaluate to true or false, making it difficult to determine the actual execution path, while \textit{Dead Code} is never invoked. 

