\keywords{Bacterial growth laws $|$ Ribogenesis$|$ Ribosome lifetime $|$ ribosome allocation $|$ translation speed}

\begin{abstract}
To double the cellular population of ribosomes, a fraction of the active ribosomes is allocated to synthesize ribosomal proteins. Subsequently, these ribosomal proteins enter the ribosome self-assembly process, synthesizing new ribosomes and forming the well-known ribosome autocatalytic subcycle. Neglecting ribosome lifetime and the duration of the self-assembly process, the doubling rate of all cellular biomass can be equated with the fraction of ribosomes allocated to synthesize an essential ribosomal protein times its synthesis rate. However, ribosomes have a finite lifetime, and the assembly process has a finite duration. Furthermore, the number of ribosomes is known to decrease with slow growth rates. The finite lifetime of ribosomes and the decline in their numbers present a challenge in sustaining slow growth solely through controlling the allocation of ribosomes to synthesize more ribosomal proteins. When the number of ribosomes allocated per mRNA of an essential ribosomal protein is approximately one, the resulting fluctuations in the production rate of new ribosomes increase, causing a potential risk that the actual production rate will fall below the ribosome death rate. Thus, in this regime, a significant risk of extinction of the ribosome population emerges. To mitigate this risk, we suggest that the ribosome translation speed is used as an alternative control parameter, which facilitates the maintenance of slow growth rates with a larger ribosome pool. We clarify the observed reduction in translation speed at harsh environments in E. \textit{coli} and C. \textit{Glutamicum}, explore other mitigation strategies and suggest additional falsifiable predictions of our model.
%To double the cellular population of ribosomes, a fraction of the active ribosomes is allocated to synthesize ribosomal proteins. These ribosomal proteins subsequently enter the ribosome self-assembly process, synthesizing new ribosomes and forming the well-known ribosome autocatalytic subcycle. Neglecting ribosome lifetime and the duration of the self-assembly process, the doubling rate of all cellular biomass can be equated with the fraction of ribosomes allocated to synthesize an essential ribosomal protein, multiplied by its synthesis rate. However, ribosomes have a finite lifetime, and the assembly process has a finite duration. Furthermore, the number of ribosomes is known to decrease at slow growth rates. The finite lifetime of ribosomes and the decline in their numbers present a challenge in sustaining slow growth solely through controlling the allocation of ribosomes for the synthesis of more ribosomal proteins. When the number of ribosomes allocated per mRNA of an essential ribosomal protein is approximately one, the resulting fluctuations in the production rate of new ribosomes increase, causing a potential risk that the actual production rate will fall below the ribosome death rate. Thus, in this regime, a significant risk of ribosome population extinction emerges. To mitigate this risk, we suggest ribosome elongation speed is employed as an alternative control parameter, which facilitates the maintenance of slow growth rates with a larger ribosome pool. Therefore, we clarify the observed reduction in ribosome elongation speed at slow growth rates in E. coli, explore other mitigation strategies in various single-cell organisms, and offer additional testable predictions to validate our model.
%To double the cellular population of ribosomes, a fraction of the active ribosomes is allocated to synthesize ribosomal proteins. Subsequently, these ribosomal proteins enter the ribosome self-assembly process, which synthesizes new ribosomes, thus forming the well-known ribosome autocatalytic subcycle. Neglecting ribosome lifetime and the duration of the self-assembly process, the doubling rate of all cellular biomass can be equated with the fraction of ribosomes allocated to synthesize an essential ribosomal protein multiplied by its synthesis rate. However, ribosomes have a finite lifetime, and the assembly process has a finite duration. The number of ribosomes is known to decrease with slow growth rates. Maintaining slow growth by controlling the allocation parameter becomes increasingly challenging as the average number of ribosomes per mRNA is reduced. When the number of ribosomes per mRNA of an essential ribosomal protein is on the order of one---known as the Shot noise regime---the resulting fluctuations in the synthesis rate of essential ribosomal proteins lead to a significant risk of ribosome population extinction. To mitigate the risk of extinction of the ribosome population, ribosome elongation speed is utilized as an alternative control parameter, allowing the maintenance of slow growth rates. Thus, we explain the observed reduction in ribosome elongation speed at slow growth rates in E. coli, discuss other mitigation strategies in different single-cell organisms, and provide additional falsifiable predictions to test our model.
%To double the cellular population of ribosomes, a fraction of the active ribosomes is allocated to synthesize ribosomal proteins. The ribosomal proteins subsequently enter the ribosome self-assembly process that synthesizes new ribosomes, thus forming the well-known ribosome autocatalytic subcycle. Neglecting ribosome lifetime and the duration of the self-assembly process, the doubling rate of all cellular biomass can be equated with the fraction of ribosomes allocated to synthesize an essential ribosomal protein times its synthesis rate. However, ribosomes have a finite lifetime, and the assembly process has a finite duration. Furthermore, the number of ribosomes is known to decrease with slow growth rates. In E. coli, growing at a doubling time of $100$ minutes, the number of ribosomes is decreased $12$-fold compared to a doubling time of $20$ minutes. Maintaining slow growth by controlling the allocation parameter becomes increasingly challenging as the average number of ribosomes per mRNA is reduced. In the Shot noise regime, where the number of ribosomes per mRNA of an essential ribosomal protein is on the order of one, there is a risk of underproducing this protein. The subsequent fluctuations in its synthesis increase the risk of extinction of the entire ribosome population. Furthermore, a significant increase in the risk of extinction of the cellular ribosome population emerges. We show that to maintain slow growth rates and mitigate the danger of ribosome population extinction, the ribosome translation speed has to be utilized as a control parameter. Thus, we explain the hitherto unexplained reduction in ribosome translation speed at slow growth rates in E. \textit{coli}, discuss other mitigation strategies in different single-cell organisms and provide additional falsifiable predictions to test our model.
\end{abstract}

