\title{\mbox{Making Non-Negative Polynomials into Sums of Squares}}


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{document}%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\begin{abstract}
We investigate linear operators $A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$.
We give explicit operators $A$ such that, for fixed $d\in\nset_0$ and closed $K\subseteq\rset^n$, $e^A\pos(K)_{\leq 2d}\subseteq\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}^2$.
We give an explicit operator $A$ such that $e^A\pos(\rset^n)\subseteq\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2$.
For $K\subseteq\rset^n$, we give a condition such that $A$ exists with $e^A\pos(K)\subseteq\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2$.
For $K=[0,\infty)^n$, this condition is fulfilled.
We show that, for compact $K\subseteq\rset^n$, there is no bijective linear operator $T:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ with $T\pos(K)\subseteq\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2$.
In the framework of regular Fr\'echet Lie groups and Lie algebras we investigate the linear operators $A$ such that $e^{tA}:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ is well-defined for all $t\in\rset$.
We give a three-line-proof of Stochel's Theorem.
\end{abstract}

