\section{Introduction}%%%
\label{sec:intro}%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


Let $n\in\nset$, $K\subseteq\rset^n$ be closed, and $\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ be the polynomials in $n$ variables with real coefficients.
The set
%
\[\pos(K) := \big\{ f\in\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n] \,\big|\, f\geq 0\ \text{on}\ K\big\}\]
%
of all polynomials which are non-negative on $K$ belongs to the most important structures in mathematics and applications  \cite{bochnak98,marshallPosPoly,lasserreSemiAlgOpt,scheidererRealAlgebraicGeometry}.


Let $\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2$ be the cone of sums of squares.
For a compact semi-algebraic set $K\subseteq\rset^n$ defined by finitely many polynomial inequalities $g_i(x)\geq 0$,
%
\[K := \{x\in\rset^n \,|\, g_1(x)\geq 0,\ \dots,\ g_m(x)\geq 0\},\]
%
a polynomial $p$ which is strictly positive on $K$, i.e., $p(x)>0$ for all $x\in K$, has the representation
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:schmDarst}
p = \sum_{e=(e_1,\dots,e_m)\in\{0,1\}^m} g_1^{e_1}\cdots g_m^{e_m}\cdot\sigma_e
\end{equation}
%
with $\sigma_e\in\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2$.
This was proved by Konrad Schmüdgen \cite{schmud91}.
Another representation is
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:putDarst}
p = \sigma_0 + \sum_{e=1}^m g_e\cdot \sigma_e
\end{equation}
%
with $\sigma_e\in\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2$
which was proved by Mihai Putinar \cite{putina93}.
See \cite{bochnak98,marshallPosPoly,scheidererRealAlgebraicGeometry} for more details.


Both representations (\ref{eq:schmDarst}) and (\ref{eq:putDarst}) only hold for $p>0$ on $K$.
If
%
\[\min_{x\in K} p_i(x) \quad\xrightarrow{i\to\infty}\quad 0,\]
%
then
%
\[\max_e \deg \sigma_{i,e} \quad\xrightarrow{i\to\infty}\quad\infty\]
%
even when $p_i\to p$ and $\deg p_i, \deg p \leq d < \infty$.
This degree blow up causes serious troubles in optimization, since even when an optimum is attained at a polynomial $p$ of finite degree, the implementation using (\ref{eq:schmDarst}) or (\ref{eq:putDarst}) blows up.
Consequently, even in the simplest case of calculating
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:minLp}
\min_{p\in S} L(p)
\end{equation}
%
with a linear functional $L:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}\to\rset$ and $S\subseteq\pos(K)_{\leq d}$ the semi-definite program with (\ref{eq:schmDarst}) or (\ref{eq:putDarst}) must in general exceed the finite degree $d$ of the problem (\ref{eq:minLp}).
Several works investigated these degree blow ups, see e.g.\ \cite{power01,schweig04,nie07,deklerk17,laurent23,baldi23,baldi24} and references therein.
Hence, it is clear that the representations (\ref{eq:schmDarst}) and (\ref{eq:putDarst}) are broken beyond repair.
And what is broken and can not be fixed must be replaced.


In the current work we investigate linear operators
%
\[A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\]
%
especially in
%
\[e^{tA} := \sum_{k\in\nset_0} \frac{t^k\cdot A^k}{k!}:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\]
%
with $t\in\rset$.
The operators $A$ are investigated to determine if a given set $S\subseteq\pos(K)$ can be moved by $e^{A}$ to a nicer set $e^{A}S$, e.g.\ in the sums of squares.
This results in a transformation of the problem (\ref{eq:minLp}) to
%
\[\min_{p\in S} L(p) = \min_{p\in e^{A}S} \tilde{L}(p)\]
%
with the linear functional $\tilde{L}:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset$ defined by $\tilde{L}(p) := L(e^{-A}p)$.




\paragraph{Structure of the Paper}
In the following \Cref{sec:prelim} we give short preliminaries of Fr\'echet spaces, LF-spaces, and regular Fr\'echet Lie groups to make the work as self-contained as possible.
In \Cref{sec:posToSoS} we present our main results (Theorems \ref{thm:main}, \ref{thm:main2}, and \ref{thm:main4}, and \Cref{cor:main2}), how to make non-negative polynomials into sums of squares, and how this is applied to semi-definite optimization (\Cref{cor:makingSoS}).
In \Cref{thm:main4} we show that, for compact $K\subseteq\rset^n$, there is no bijective linear operator $T:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ with $T\pos(K)\subseteq\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2$.
In \Cref{sec:fg} we investigate the set $\fg$ of all linear maps $A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ such that $e^{tA}$ is well-defined, especially the structure of regular Fr\'echet Lie algebras in $\fg$.
In \Cref{sec:stochel} we give a three-line-proof of Stochel's Theorem \cite{stochel01}.
In \Cref{sec:open} we give an open problem.



\section{Preliminaries}%%%
\label{sec:prelim}%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


For a set $S$ we denote by $\inter S$ its interior.


A Fr\'echet space is a metrizable, complete, and locally convex topological vector space \cite{treves67}.
Our leading example is the set of real sequences $\rset^{\nset_0^n}$.
Here, the topology is generated by the semi-norms $\|\cdot\|_d$ defined by
%
\[\|s\|_d := \max_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n:|\alpha|\leq d} |s_\alpha|\]
%
for every $d\in\nset_0$ and sequence $s=(s_\alpha)_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n}$ with $|\alpha|:= \alpha_1 + \dots + \alpha_n$ for all $\alpha = (\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_n)\in\nset_0^n$.
To understand the Fr\'echet topology of $\rset^{\nset_0^n}$ it is sufficient to understand the convergence in this space.
Let $s^{(k)} = (s_{k,\alpha})_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n}$ for all $k\in\nset$.
Then
%
\[s^{(k)} \xrightarrow{k\to\infty} s \qquad\text{iff}\qquad s_{k,\alpha}\xrightarrow{k\to\infty} s_\alpha\ \text{for all}\ \alpha\in\nset_0^n,\]
%
i.e., $\rset^{\nset_0^n}$ has the coordinate-wise convergence.


The space of polynomials $\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ is a LF-space and its topology can be described by the convergence of $(p_k)_{k\in\nset}\subseteq\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$:
%
\[p_k\xrightarrow{k\to\infty} p \qquad\text{iff}\qquad \sup_{k\in\nset} \deg p_k = D < \infty\ \text{and}\ p_k\to p\ \text{in}\ \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq D}.\]


The space $\rset^{\nset_0^n}$ and $\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ are dual to each other with the pairing
%
\[\langle s,p\rangle := L_s(p)\]
%
where $s\in\rset^{\nset_0^n}$, $p\in\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$, and $L_s$ is the Riesz functional
%
\[L_s:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset,\qquad L_s(x^\alpha) := s_\alpha.\]


All linear operators $T:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ have a representation
%
\[T = \sum_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n} q_\alpha\cdot\partial^\alpha\]
%
with unique coefficients $q_\alpha\in\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$.
A sequence $(T_k)_{k\in\nset}$ of such linear operators converges to a linear operator $T$ iff $q_{k,\alpha}\to q_\alpha$ for all $\alpha\in\nset_0^n$ or equivalently iff $T_kp \to Tp$ for all $p\in\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$.


Since $\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ is a nuclear space, by the Schwartz Kernel Theorem \cite[Ch.\ 51]{treves67}, every linear operator $T:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ also has the representation
%
\[T = \sum_{i\in\nset_0} l_i\cdot f_i\]
%
with linear functionals $l_i:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset$ and $f_i\in\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ for all $i\in\nset_0$.


We assume the reader has a basic knowledge about Lie groups and Lie algebras \cite{warner83}.
We only give the definition of their infinite dimensional version.


\begin{dfn}[see e.g.\ {\cite[p.\ 63, Dfn.\ 1.1]{omori97}}]\label{dfn:regFLieGroup}
We call $(G,\,\cdot\,)$ a \emph{regular Fr\'echet Lie group} if the following conditions are fulfilled:
%
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item $G$ is an infinite dimensional smooth Fr\'echet manifold.

\item $(G,\,\cdot\,)$ is a group.

\item The map $G\times G\to G$, $(A,B)\mapsto A\cdot B^{-1}$ is smooth.

\item The \emph{Fr\'echet Lie algebra} $\fg$ of $G$ is isomorphic to the tangent space $T_eG$ of $G$ at the unit element $e\in G$.

\item $\exp:\fg\to G$ is a smooth mapping such that
%
\[\frac{\diff}{\diff t} \exp(tu) \big|_{t=0} = u\]
%
for all $u\in\fg$.

\item The space $C^1(G,\fg)$ of $C^1$-curves in $G$ coincides with the set of all $C^1$-curves in $G$ under the Fr\'echet topology.
\end{enumerate}
\end{dfn}


For more on infinite dimensional calculus, regular Fr\'echet Lie groups, and their Lie algebras see e.g.\
\cite{omori74,omori97,schmed23}.



\section{Making Non-Negative Polynomials into Sums of Squares}%%%
\label{sec:posToSoS}%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


The following is our first main result.


\begin{thm}\label{thm:main}
Let $n\in\nset$, $d\in\nset_0$, $S\subseteq\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}$, and let $C\subseteq\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}$ be a full dimensional cone such that there is a linear functional
%
\[l:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}\to\rset \qquad\text{with}\qquad l(p)>0 \quad\text{for all}\quad p\in (S\cup C)\setminus\{0\}.\]
%
If $f\in\inter C$ and the linear operator $A$ is defined by
%
\[A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d},\quad p\mapsto Ap := l(p)\cdot f,\]
%
then there exists a constant $\tau = \tau(f,C,S,l)>0$ such that
%
\[e^{tA}S \quad\subseteq\quad C\]
%
for all $t\geq\tau$.
\end{thm}
%
\begin{proof}
Let
%
\[\tilde{S} := \overline{\cone \conv S}\]
%
be the closed convex cone generated by $S$.
Since $l(p)>0$ for all $p\in S$, $\tilde{S}$ is closed and pointed, i.e., $\tilde{S}$ has a compact base $B$.


Let $p\in B$ and $f\in\inter C$, i.e., $l(p)>0$ and $l(f)>0$.
Then
%
\[Ap = l(p)\cdot f \qquad\text{and}\qquad A^k p = l(p)\cdot l(f)^{k-1}\cdot f\]
%
for all $k\in\nset$.
Hence,
%
\[
e^{tA} p = \sum_{k\in\nset_0} \frac{t^k}{k!}\cdot A^k p
= p + l(p)\cdot\sum_{k\in\nset} \frac{t^k}{k!}\cdot l(f)^{k-1}\cdot f
= p + \frac{l(p)}{l(f)}\cdot \left(e^{t\cdot l(f)}-1\right)\cdot f
\]
%
and
%
\[\frac{l(f)}{l(p)}\cdot \left(e^{t\cdot l(f)}-1\right)^{-1}\cdot e^{tA}p \quad\xrightarrow{t\to\infty}\quad f.\]
%
Since
%
\[\frac{l(f)}{l(p)}\cdot \left(e^{t\cdot l(f)}-1\right)^{-1}\cdot e^{tA}p\]
%
is continuous in $t$ and $p$ and since $f$ is an inner point of $C$, there exists a neighborhood $U\subseteq C$ of $f$ and a constant $\tau\geq 0$ such that
%
\[e^{tA}S \quad\subseteq\quad e^{tA}\tilde{S} \quad\subseteq\quad \cone U \quad\subseteq\quad C\]
%
for all $t\geq\tau$.
\end{proof}


In the previous theorem it is clear that the main space $\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}$ can be replaced by any Banach space $\cV$.
For special and important cases of $S$ and $C$ in \Cref{thm:main} we get the following corollary.


\begin{cor}\label{cor:makingSoS}
Let $n\in\nset$, $d\in\nset_0$, $K\subseteq\rset^n$ be closed, and let $S\subseteq\pos(K)_{\leq 2d}$.
The following statements hold:
%
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item There exists a linear operator
%
\[A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\]
%
and a constant $\tau\geq 0$ such that
%
\[e^{tA}S \quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\]
%
for all $t\geq\tau$.

\item Let
%
\[f\in\inter \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}^2\]
%
and let
%
\[l:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\to\rset,\quad l(p):= \int_K p(x)\cdot e^{-x^2}~\diff x.\]
%
Then
%
\[A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}, \quad p\mapsto Ap := l(p)\cdot f\]
%
is an operator as in (i).

\item Let
%
\[L:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\to\rset\]
%
be a linear functional and let $A$ be an operator as in (i).
Set
%
\[\tilde{S} := e^{\tau A}S\subseteq\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}^2\]
%
and
%
\[\tilde{L}:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\to\rset,\quad p\mapsto \tilde{L}(p) := L(e^{-\tau A}p).\]
%
Then
%
\[\inf_{p\in S} L(p) = \inf_{p\in \tilde{S}} \tilde{L}(p) \qquad\text{and}\qquad \sup_{p\in S} L(p) = \sup_{p\in \tilde{S}} \tilde{L}(p).\]
\end{enumerate}
\end{cor}
%
\begin{proof}
(i) + (ii): Since $C=\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}^2$ is a full dimensional cone and $l(p)>0$ for all $p\in (S\cup C)\setminus\{0\}$, \Cref{thm:main} gives the assertion.

(iii): Follows from the identity $e^{-\tau A} e^{\tau A} = \one$.
\end{proof}


\begin{rem}
An equivalent results as in \Cref{cor:makingSoS} for the sums of squares cone also holds for the Waring cone by \Cref{thm:main}.
\exmsymbol
\end{rem}


Since \Cref{cor:makingSoS} contains the degree bound $\leq 2d$, it can also be interpreted to work on the homogeneous polynomials:
%
\[\rset[x_0,x_1,\dots,x_n]_{=2d}\quad \cong\quad \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}.\]


Note, the construction of the operator $A$ in \Cref{thm:main} and \Cref{cor:makingSoS} is very specific, since $A$ is made in such a way that $f\cdot [0,\infty)$ becomes an accumulation ray, i.e., with increasing time $t$ every $e^{tA}p$ converges towards the ray spanned by $f$.
This requires the degree bounds in these results.
%Hence, to overcome this drawback, we have to learn more about the maps $A$ such that $e^{tA}$ is well-defined.
In the following theorem the degree restriction is removed for $K=\rset^n$.
It is our second main result.


\begin{thm}\label{thm:main2}
Let $n\in\nset$.
The following statements hold:
%
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item There exists a linear operator
%
\[A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\]
%
with
%
\[A\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\quad\subseteq\quad\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\]
%
for all $d\in\nset_0$ such that
%
\[e^A\pos(\rset^n)_{\leq 2d}\quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}^2\]
%
for all $d\in\nset_0$ and therefore
%
\[e^A\pos(\rset^n)\quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2.\]

\item For $d\in\nset_0$, define the linear operators $l_d:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset$ by
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ldRn}
p=\sum_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n} p_\alpha x^\alpha\quad\mapsto\quad l_d(p) := \int_{\rset^n} e^{-x^2}\cdot\sum_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n:|\alpha|=2d} p_\alpha x^\alpha~\diff x.
\end{equation}
%
Then for any sequence $(f_d)_{d\in\nset_0}\subseteq\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ of polynomials with
%
\[\deg f_d = 2d \quad\text{and}\quad f_d\in\inter \sum \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}^2\]
%
there exist constants $c_0,c_1,c_2,{\dots} > 0$ such that the operator $A$ in (i) is of the form
%
\[A = \sum_{d\in\nset_0} c_d\cdot l_d\cdot f_d, \qquad\text{i.e.,}\qquad Ap = \sum_{d\in\nset_0} c_d\cdot l_d(p)\cdot f_d\]
%
for all $p\in\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$.
\end{enumerate}


\end{thm}
%
\begin{proof}
It is sufficient to prove (ii).
We prove the statement via induction over the degree $d$.


\underline{$d=0$:}
Since
%
\[\pos(\rset^n)_{\leq d=0} = \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d=0}^2 = [0,\infty)\cdot 1,\]
%
we have after rescaling $f_0 = 1$ and we can set $c_0 = 1$ to get
%
\[A_0 := l_0\cdot 1.\]

\underline{$d\to d+1$:}
For $d\in\nset_0$, assume we have an operator
%
\[A_d = \sum_{k=0}^d c_k\cdot l_k\cdot f_k\]
%
with
%
\[A_d\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2k} \quad\subseteq\quad \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2k}\]
%
and
%
\[e^A\pos(\rset^n)_{\leq 2k} \quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq k}^2\]
%
for all $k=0,1,\dots,d$.
The operator
%
\[A_d:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d+2} \to \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d+2}\]
%
is a bounded operator.
Furthermore, $\pos(\rset^n)_{\leq 2d+2}$ is a pointed cone with compact base.
For any
%
\[p = \sum_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n} p_\alpha x^\alpha \in\pos(\rset^n)\]
%
with $\deg p = 2d+2$ the homogeneous part
%
\[p|_{=2d+2} = \sum_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n: |\alpha|=2d+2} p_\alpha\cdot x^\alpha\]
%
is non-negative, i.e., $p|_{=2d+2}\geq 0$ on $\rset^n$, and therefore $l_{d+1}(p) > 0$.
Hence, for
%
\[A_{d+1} := A_d + c_{d+1}\cdot l_{d+1}\cdot f_{d+1} = \sum_{k=0}^{d+1} c_k\cdot l_k\cdot f_k\]
%
there exists a $c_{d+1}\gg 0$ such that
%
\[e^{A_{d+1}}\pos(\rset^n)_{\leq 2d+2} \quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d+1}^2,\]
%
since
%
\[f_{d+1}\in \inter \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d+1}^2\]
%
and
%
\[e^{A_d + c_{d+1}\cdot l_{d+1}\cdot f_{d+1}}:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d+2}\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d+2}\]
%
is continuous in $c_{d+1}$.
That is the same fixed-ray argument as in \Cref{thm:main} and $A_d$ is only a perturbation to $c_{d+1}\cdot l_{d+1}\cdot f_{d+1}$, since $c_{d+1}\gg c_0,\dots,c_d$.


Now, set
%
\[A := \sum_{d\in\nset_0} c_d\cdot l_d\cdot f_d.\]
%
Then $A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ is a well-defined linear operator, since $l_D(p) = 0$ for all $p\in\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ and $D\in\nset_0$ such that $\deg p < 2D$, i.e., the sum
%
\[Ap = \sum_{d\in\nset_0} c_d\cdot l_d(p)\cdot f_d\]
%
contains only finitely many non-zero terms.
This proves (ii).
\end{proof}


\begin{rem}
In the previous theorem we can, of course, rescale the given
%
\[f_d = \sum_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n} f_{d,\alpha}\cdot x^\alpha \in\inter\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}^2.\]
%
Hence, we can assume without loss of generality
%
\[\sum_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n} f_{d,\alpha}^2 = 1.\]
%
Then, in general,
%
\[0 \quad\ll\quad c_0 \quad\ll\quad c_1 \quad\ll\quad {\dots} \quad\ll\quad c_d \quad\ll\quad {\dots}\ .\]
%
This increase in the constants $c_d$ reflects the fact that ``there are significantly more non-negative polynomials than sums of squares'' \cite{blekh06}.
\exmsymbol
\end{rem}


\begin{rem}\label{rem:main2}
From \Cref{thm:main2} we find that given any
%
\[S\subseteq\pos(\rset^n)\]
%
and any linear functional
%
\[L:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset,\]
%
the optimization problems
%
\[\inf_{p\in S} L(p) \qquad\text{and}\qquad \sup_{p\in S} L(p)\]
%
transform into optimization problems
%
\[\inf_{p\in S} L(p) \ = \ \inf_{p\in e^A S} \tilde{L}(p)
%
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
%
\sup_{p\in S} L(p) \ = \ \sup_{p\in e^A S} \tilde{L}(p)\]
%
over the subset $e^AS$ of sums of squares $\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2$ with
%
\[\tilde{L}: \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset,\quad p\mapsto \tilde{L}(p) := L(e^{-A}p).\]
%
Here, $A\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d} \subseteq\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}$ for all $d\in\nset_0$ implies
%
\[e^A \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d} \quad\subseteq\quad \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\]
%
for all $d\in\nset_0$, i.e., no additional degree blow up is introduced and also the Lasserre hierarchy can be used.
\exmsymbol
\end{rem}


A close look at the proof of \Cref{thm:main2} reveals that the operator $A$ in \Cref{thm:main2} (ii) can be constructed because the $l_d$ are given by (\ref{eq:ldRn}).
If similar linear functionals exist for $\pos(K)$ for a closed $K\subseteq\rset^n$, then we get the following.


\begin{cor}\label{cor:main2}
Let $n\in\nset$ and let $K\subseteq\rset^n$ be closed.
Assume there exist
%
\[0\quad\leq\quad d_0 \quad<\quad d_1 \quad<\quad d_2 \quad<\quad {\dots}\]
%
in $\nset_0$ and linear functionals
%
$l_i:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset$
%
with
%
\begin{align*}
l_i(p) &> 0 &&\text{for all}\quad p\in\pos(K)_{\leq 2d_i}\setminus\pos(K)_{\leq 2d_{i-1}}
%
\intertext{and}
%
l_i(x^\alpha) &= 0 &&\text{for all}\quad \alpha\in\nset_0^n\ \text{with}\ |\alpha|> 2d_i
\end{align*}
%
for all $i\in\nset_0$.
Then for any sequence $(f_i)_{i\in\nset_0}$ of polynomials with
%
\[\deg f_i = 2d_i \quad\text{and}\quad f_i\in\inter\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d_i}^2\]
%
there exist constants
%
\[0 \quad<\quad c_0 \quad<\quad c_1 \quad<\quad c_2 \quad<\quad {\dots}\]
%
such that the linear operator
%
\[A = \sum_{i\in\nset_0} c_i\cdot l_i\cdot f_i: \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\]
%
is well-defined with
%
\[e^A\pos(K)_{\leq 2d_i} \quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d_i}^2\]
%
for all $i\in\nset_0$ and therefore
%
\[e^A\pos(K) \quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2.\]
\end{cor}
%
\begin{proof}
Proceed as in the proof of \Cref{thm:main2}.
\end{proof}


In \Cref{thm:main2} and \Cref{cor:main2} the conditions
%
\[\deg f_d = 2d \qquad\text{and}\qquad \deg f_i = 2d_i\]
%
are already implied by
%
\[f_d\in\inter\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}^2 \qquad\text{and}\qquad f_i\in\inter\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d_i}^2.\]
%
However, they are included for clarity.


The conditions in \Cref{cor:main2} are fulfilled if $K\subseteq\rset^n$ contains a full-dimensional cone.


\begin{thm}\label{thm:main3}
Let $n\in\nset$, let $K\subseteq\rset^n$, and let $(f_d)_{d\in\nset_0}$ be a sequence of polynomials with
%
\[\deg f_d = 2d \quad\text{and}\quad f_d\in\inter\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2\]
%
for all $d\in\nset$.
If $K$ contains a full-dimensional cone $G$, then define
%
\[l_d:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset,\quad \sum_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n} p_\alpha\cdot x^\alpha\mapsto \int_G e^{-x^2}\cdot \sum_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n:|\alpha|=2d} p_\alpha x_\alpha~\diff x\]
%
for all $d\in\nset_0$ and
%
\[\tilde{l}_d:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset,\quad \sum_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n} p_\alpha\cdot x^\alpha\mapsto \int_G e^{-x^2}\cdot \sum_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n:|\alpha|=2d-1} p_\alpha x_\alpha~\diff x,\]
%
for all $d\in\nset$, and then there exist coefficients $c_0,\tilde{c}_1,c_1,\tilde{c}_2,c_2,{\dots}\geq 0$ such that
%
\[A := \sum_{k=0}^\infty c_k\cdot l_k\cdot f_k + \sum_{k=1}^\infty \tilde{c}_k\cdot \tilde{l}_k\cdot f_k\]
%
fulfills
%
\[e^{A}\pos(K)_{\leq 2d-1} \quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}^2\]
%
for all $d\in\nset$, and
%
\[e^{A}\pos(K)_{\leq 2d} \quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}^2\]
%
for all $d\in\nset_0$, i.e.,
%
\[e^{A}\pos(K) \quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2.\]
\end{thm}
%
\begin{proof}
We proceed as in \Cref{thm:main2} by induction over the degree $d$.
By taking only a full-dimensional subcone $B$ of $G$ spanned by the linearly independent vectors $b_1,\dots,b_n$ and then by a linear variable transformation $x_1,\dots,x_n$ to $\tilde{x}_1,\dots,\tilde{x}_n$ such that $\tilde{b}_1,\dots,\tilde{b}_n$ are the standard unit vectors of $\rset^n$, we can assume without loss of generality that $G=[0,\infty)^n$.


\underline{For $d=0$}, let $A_0 := l_0\cdot 1$.
%
Then
%
\[e^{A_0}\pos(K)_{\leq 0} = \pos(K)_{\leq 0} = [0,\infty)\cdot 1 = \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 0}^2.\]


\underline{Assume, for $d\in\nset_0$}, we already have an operator
%
\[A_d:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\]
%
with
%
\[A_d = \sum_{k=0}^d c_k\cdot l_k\cdot f_k + \sum_{k=1}^d \tilde{c}_k\cdot \tilde{l}_k\cdot f_k\]
%
such that
%
\[e^{A_d}\pos(K)_{\leq 2k-1} \quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq k}^2\]
%
for all $k=1,\dots,d$, and
%
\[e^{A_d}\pos(K)_{\leq 2k} \quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq k}^2\]
%
for all $k=0,\dots,d$.
Let $p\in\pos(K)$ with $\deg p = 2d+1$, i.e.,
%
\[p = \sum_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n:|\alpha|\leq 2d+1} p_\alpha x^\alpha.\]
%
Then
%
\[p|_{2d+1} = \sum_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n:|\alpha|=2d+1} p_\alpha x^\alpha \geq 0\]
%
on $K$.
Let $f_{d+1}\in\inter\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d+1}^2$ with
%
\[\tilde{l}_{d+1}(f_{d+1}) > 0 \quad\text{and}\quad l_{d+1}(f_{d+1})>0.\]
%
Since $e^{A_d+\tilde{c}_{d+1}\tilde{l}_{d+1} f_{d+1}}$ is continuous in $\tilde{c}_{d+1}$ and since $\pos(K)_{\leq 2d+1}$ is pointed and closed, i.e., it has a compact base, there exists a $\tilde{c}_{d+1}\geq 0$ such that
%
\[e^{A_d + \tilde{c}_{d+1} \tilde{l}_{d+1} f_{d+1}}\pos(K)_{\leq 2d+1} \quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d+1}^2.\]
%
Since $e^{A_d+\tilde{c}_{d+1}\tilde{l}_{d+1} f_{d+1} + c_{d+1} l_{d+1} f_{d+1}}$ is continuous in $c_{d+1}$, $\pos(K)_{\leq 2d+2}$ is closed and pointed, i.e., has a compact base, there exists a $c_{d+1}\geq 0$ such that
%
\[\tilde{c}_{d+1} \tilde{l}_{d+1}(p) + c_{d+1} l_{d+1}(p)\geq 0\]
%
for all $p\in\pos(K)_{\leq 2d+2}$ and
%
\[e^{A_d + (\tilde{c}_{d+1} \tilde{l}_{d+1} + c_{d+1} l_{d+1})\cdot f_{d+1}}\pos(K)_{\leq 2d+2} \quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d+1}^2.\]


At last, set
%
\[A := \sum_{k=0}^\infty c_k\cdot l_k\cdot f_k + \sum_{k=1}^\infty \tilde{c}_k\cdot \tilde{l}_k\cdot f_k.\]
%
Then
%
\[A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\]
%
is well-defined, since, for every $p\in\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$, $\deg p<\infty$ and hence $Ap$ contains only finitely many non-zero terms.
%
Since
%
\[A\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\quad \subseteq\quad \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\]
%
for all $d\in\nset_0$, $e^A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ is well-defined with
%
\[e^A\pos(K)_{\leq 2d-1} \quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}^2\]
%
for all $d\in\nset$,
%
\[e^A\pos(K)_{\leq 2d} \quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d}^2\]
%
for all $d\in\nset_0$, and hence
%
\[e^A\pos(K) \quad\subseteq\quad \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2\]
%
which ends the proof.
\end{proof}


If the assumptions in \Cref{cor:main2} are fulfilled, then an equivalent remark as \Cref{rem:main2} holds for $\pos(K)$ instead of $\pos(\rset^n)$.


\begin{rem}
If in \Cref{thm:main2} (or \Cref{cor:main2} or \Cref{thm:main3}) each $f_d$ is in the interior of the Waring cone, i.e., the cone of even powers of linear forms of degree $2d$, then there exists a linear operator $A$ as in \Cref{thm:main2} (resp.\ \Cref{cor:main2}) such that
%
$e^A\pos(\rset^n)$ (resp.\ $e^A\pos(K)$)
%
is degree preserving and is a subset of the Waring cone.
\exmsymbol
\end{rem}


\Cref{thm:main3} does not hold for compact $K\subseteq\rset^n$.
The following \Cref{thm:main4} shows that, for compact $K\subseteq\rset^n$, there exists no linear operator
%
\[A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\]
%
such that
%
\[e^A\pos(K)\ \subseteq\ \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2.\]
%
\Cref{thm:main4} is even stronger: There exists no bijective linear operator
%
\[T:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\]
%
with
%
\[T\pos(K)\ \subseteq\ \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2.\]


\begin{thm}\label{thm:main4}
Let $n\in\nset$ and let $K\subseteq\rset^n$ be compact.
There exists no bijective and linear operator
%
\[T:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\]
%
such that
%
\[T\pos(K)\ \subseteq\ \sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2.\]
\end{thm}
%
\begin{proof}
Assume there exists a bijective linear operator
%
\[T:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n] \quad\text{with}\quad T\pos(K)\subseteq\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2.\]
%
Since $T$ is bijective, there exist $p\in\pos(K)$ and $c>0$ with
%
\[c-p\in\pos(K)\qquad \text{and}\qquad \deg Tp\ >\ \deg T1.\]
%
Then
%
\[Tc = T(p + c-p) = Tp + T(c-p)\]
%
with
%
\[Tc,\ Tp,\ T(c-p) \quad\in\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2\]
%
implies
%
\[\deg Tc\ =\ \max\{\deg Tp,\ \deg T(c-p)\}\ \geq\ \deg Tp\ >\ \deg Tc\]
%
which is a contradiction.
\end{proof}



\section{The set $\fg$ and its regular Fr\'echet Lie Algebras}%%%
\label{sec:fg}%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


In \Cref{thm:main} and \Cref{cor:makingSoS} we gave explicit operators
%
\[A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\]
%
such that certain given sets $S$ are moved by $e^{tA}$ to the nicer sets $e^{tA}S$, e.g.\ subsets of the sums of squares cone or the Waring cone.
These results are restricted to the degrees $d$ and $2d$, i.e., for every $d\in\nset$ another operator $A$ and constant $\tau$ in \Cref{cor:makingSoS} (iii) must be calculated.
In \Cref{thm:main2} we gave an explicit linear operator $A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ with
%
\[e^A\pos(\rset^n)\subseteq\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2.\]
%
For $\pos(K)$ with closed $K\subseteq\rset^n$ we needed additional assumptions in \Cref{cor:main2}.
For compact $K\subseteq\rset^n$, in \Cref{thm:main4} we show that no operator $A$ with
%
\[e^A\pos(K)\subseteq\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2\]
%
exists.
We therefore study now linear operators
%
\[A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\]
%
such that
%
\[e^{tA}:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\]
%
is well-defined for all $t\in\rset$.


\begin{dfn}\label{dfn:fg}
Let $n\in\nset$.
We define
%
\begin{multline*}
\fg := \Big\{A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\ \text{linear} \,\Big|\\ e^{tA}:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\
\text{well-defined for all}\ t\in\rset\Big\}.
\end{multline*}
\end{dfn}


It is sufficient in the \Cref{dfn:fg} of $\fg$ that $e^{tA}$ is well-defined as a linear map $\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ only for all $t\in (-\varepsilon,\varepsilon)$ for some $\varepsilon>0$.


Clearly,
%
\[\fg = \rset\cdot\fg,\]
%
i.e., $\fg$ is a cone and $\fg = -\fg$.


However, $\fg$ is not convex and it is not a vector space, i.e., there are $A,B\in\fg$ such that $A+B\notin\fg$.
We have the following example of such maps $A$ and $B$.


\begin{exm}\label{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd}
Let $n=1$.
Define the linear operators
%
\[A:\rset[x]\to\rset[x] \qquad\text{and}\qquad B:\rset[x]\to\rset[x]\]
%
by
%
\[Ax^k := \begin{cases}
x^k & \text{for}\ k=2m\ \text{with}\ m\in\nset_0,\\
x^{k+1} & \text{for}\ k=2m-1\ \text{with}\ m\in\nset
\end{cases}\]
%
and
%
\[Bx^k := \begin{cases}
x^{k+1} & \text{for}\ k=2m\ \text{with}\ m\in\nset_0,\\
x^k & \text{for}\ k=2m-1\ \text{with}\ m\in\nset
\end{cases}\]
%
for all $k\in\nset_0$ with linear extension to all $\rset[x]$.
Then
%
\[A\rset[x]_{\leq 2m}\ \subseteq\ \rset[x]_{\leq 2m} \qquad\text{and}\qquad B\rset[x]_{\leq 2m+1}\ \subseteq\ \rset[x]_{\leq 2m+1}\]
%
for all $m\in\nset_0$.
Since $A|_{\rset[x]_{\leq 2m}}$ and $B|_{\rset[x]_{\leq 2m+1}}$ are linear operators on finite dimensional spaces, i.e., matrices, $e^{tA}$ and $e^{tB}$ are well-defined for every $p\in\rset[x]$ and hence they are well-defined as linear maps $\rset[x]\to\rset[x]$: $A,B\in\fg$.
However,
%
\[(A+B)x^k\quad =\quad \begin{cases}
x^k + x^{k+1} & \text{for}\ k=2m\ \text{with}\ m\in\nset_0,\\
x^{k+1} + x^k & \text{for}\ k=2m-1\ \text{with}\ m\in\nset
\end{cases}\quad =\quad x^k + x^{k+1}\]
%
for all $k\in\nset_0$, i.e.,
%
\[\deg \left(\sum_{i=0}^d \frac{t^i}{i!}\cdot (A+B)^i 1\right) = d \quad\xrightarrow{d\to\infty}\quad\infty\]
%
for all $t\neq 0$.
Hence, $e^{t(A+B)}$ is not a map $\rset[x]\to\rset[x]$ and $A+B\notin\fg$.
\exmsymbol
\end{exm}


The previous example showed that if for a linear operator
%
\[A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\]
%
there exist $d_0 < d_1 < d_2 < \dots$ in $\nset_0$ with
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:invariantDegree}
A\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d_i}\ \subseteq\ \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq d_i}
\end{equation}
%
for all $i\in\nset_0$, then $A\in\fg$.
This was the property used in \cite{didio24posPresConst,didio24posPres2arxiv}.
The next examples shows that (\ref{eq:invariantDegree}) is sufficient, but (\ref{eq:invariantDegree}) is not necessary.


\begin{exm}
Let $n=1$ and let $(p_i)_{i\in\nset}= (2,3,5,7,11,\dots)$ be the list of prime numbers.
Define the linear map $A:\rset[x]\to\rset[x]$ by
%
\[Ax^k := \begin{cases}
x^{p_{m+1}} & \text{for}\ k=2m\ \text{with}\ m\in\nset,\\
0 & \text{else}.
\end{cases}\]
%
Then
%
\[A\rset[x]_{\leq d}\ \not\subseteq\ \rset[x]_{\leq d}\]
%
for all $d\in\nset$ with $d\geq 8$.
However,
%
\[A1 = 0, \quad Ax = 0, \quad Ax^2 = x^{p_2} = x^3,\quad Ax^3 = 0,\quad Ax^4 = x^{p_3} = x^5,\quad \dots\]
%
and therefore
%
\[A^2 x^k = 0\]
%
for all $k\in\nset_0$, i.e., $e^{tA}:\rset[x]\to\rset[x]$ is well-defined for all $t\in\rset$ and $A\in\fg$.
\exmsymbol
\end{exm}


The following theorem gives characterizations of $\fg$.


\begin{thm}\label{thm:gCharac}
Let $n\in\nset$ and let
%
\[A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\]
%
be linear.
The following are equivalent:
%
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item $A\in\fg$.

\item $\displaystyle \sup_{k\in\nset_0} \deg A^k x^\alpha < \infty$ for all $\alpha\in\nset_0^n$.

\item For all $i\in\nset_0$, there exist subspaces $V_i\subseteq\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ with
%
\begin{enumerate}[(a)]
\item $\dim V_i < \infty$,

\item $\displaystyle \bigcup_{i\in\nset_0} V_i = \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$, and

\item $AV_i \subseteq V_i$ for all $i\in\nset_0$.
\end{enumerate}

\end{enumerate}
\end{thm}
%
\begin{proof}
The implications ``(ii) $\Rightarrow$ (i)'' and ``(iii) $\Rightarrow$ (i)'' are clear.


(i) $\Rightarrow$ (ii):
%
Since $e^{tA}$ is well-defined, for each $\alpha\in\nset_0^n$, there exists a degree $D=D(\alpha)\in\nset_0^n$ such that
%
\[e^{tA}x^\alpha\ \subseteq\ \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq D}\]
%
for all $t\in\rset$.
Hence,
%
\[A^k x^\alpha\ =\ \partial^k_t e^{tA}x^\alpha \Big|_{t=0}\ \in\ \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq D}\]
%
for all $k\in\nset_0$, which proves (ii).


[(i) $\Leftrightarrow$ (ii)] $\Rightarrow$ (iii):
%
Let $\alpha\in\nset_0^n$.
By (i) and (ii), there exists a $D=D(\alpha)\in\nset_0$ with
%
\[e^{tA}x^\alpha,\ A^k x^\alpha\ \in\ \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq D}\]
%
for all $k\in\nset_0$ and all $t\in\rset$.
Set
%
\[V_{\alpha,0} := \rset\cdot x^\alpha.\]
%
By (ii),
%
\[AV_{\alpha,0}\quad \subseteq\quad \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq D}.\]
%
For all $i\in\nset_0$, define
%
\[V_{\alpha,i+1} := V_{\alpha,i} + AV_{\alpha,i},\]
%
i.e.,
%
\[V_{\alpha,k}=\rset\cdot x^\alpha +\rset\cdot Ax^\alpha +\dots+\rset\cdot A^k x^\alpha\]
%
for all $k\in\nset_0$.
By (ii) and the definition of $V_{\alpha,i+1}$,
%
\[V_{\alpha,i} \quad\subseteq\quad V_{\alpha,i+1} \quad\subseteq\quad \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq D}\]
%
and hence
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:increasingViSequence}
V_{\alpha,0} \quad\subseteq\quad V_{\alpha,1} \quad\subseteq\quad \dots \quad\subseteq\quad \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq D}.
\end{equation}
%
Since (\ref{eq:increasingViSequence}) is an increasing sequence of finite dimensional vector spaces $V_{\alpha,i}$ bounded from above by the finite dimensional vector space $\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq D}$, there exists an index $I(\alpha)\in\nset_0$ such that
%
\[V_{\alpha,I(\alpha)} = V_{\alpha,I(\alpha)+1},\]
%
i.e.,
%
\[\dim V_{\alpha,I(\alpha)} < \infty \qquad\text{and}\qquad AV_{\alpha,I(\alpha)} \subseteq V_{\alpha,I(\alpha)}.\]
%
Since $\alpha\in\nset_0^n$ was arbitrary and $x^\alpha\in V_{\alpha,I(\alpha)}$,
%
\[\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n] = \bigcup_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n} V_{\alpha,I(\alpha)}.\]
%
Since $\nset_0^n$ is countable, we proved (iii) (a) -- (c).
\end{proof}


\Cref{thm:gCharac} tells us that $e^{tA}$ is only the matrix exponential function, since for an operator $A\in\fg$ we only need to know $A$ on every finite dimensional invariant subspace $V_i$, i.e., its calculation is easy as soon as the $V_i$ for $A$ are known.
An algorithm for the calculation of these $V_i$ is explicitly given in the proof of \Cref{thm:gCharac}.


For the invariant subspaces $V_i$ of $A\in\fg$ we have the following result.


\begin{cor}
Let $n\in\nset$, let $A\in\fg$, and let $V\subseteq\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ be a finite or infinite dimensional subspace.
The following are equivalent:
%
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item $AV\subseteq V$.

\item $e^{tA}V \subseteq V$ for all $t\in\rset$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{cor}
%
\begin{proof}
The direction ``(i) $\Rightarrow$ (ii)'' follows from $e^{tA} = \sum_{k\in\nset_0} k!^{-1}\cdot t^k\cdot A^k$ and the direction ``(ii) $\Rightarrow$ (i)'' follows from $A = \partial_t e^{tA}\big|_{t=0}$.
\end{proof}


We have seen in \Cref{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd} that $\fg$ is not closed under addition.
The following example shows that $\fg$ is also not closed under multiplication.


\begin{exm}[\Cref{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd} continued]\label{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd2}
Let $A,B:\rset[x]\to\rset[x]$ be given as in \Cref{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd}.
Then
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:AB}
AB x^{2m} = x^{2m+2},\qquad ABx^{2m+1} = x^{2m+2},
\end{equation}
%
and
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:BA}
BAx^{2m} = x^{2m+1},\qquad BA x^{2m+1} = x^{2m+3},
\end{equation}
%
for all $m\in\nset_0$, i.e., by \Cref{thm:gCharac} (ii), $AB\notin\fg$ as well as $BA\notin\fg$.
\exmsymbol
\end{exm}


If two operators $A,B\in\fg$ possess a common family $\{V_i\}_{i\in\nset_0}$ of invariant and finite dimensional subspace $V_i$ as provided by \Cref{thm:gCharac} (iii), then
%
\[A+B\in\fg, \quad AB\in\fg,\quad\text{and}\quad BA\in\fg\]
%
and the Lie bracket $[\,\cdot\,,\,\cdot\,]$ is given by
%
\[[A,B] := AB - BA\ \in\fg.\]


In general, $\fg$ is not closed under this Lie bracket.
The operators $A,B\in\fg$ in \Cref{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd} provide an example of $[A,B]\notin\fg$.


\begin{exm}[\Cref{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd} and \ref{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd2} continued]\label{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd3}
Let $A:\rset[x]\to\rset[x]$ and $B:\rset[x]\to\rset[x]$ be defined as in \Cref{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd}.
By (\ref{eq:AB}) and (\ref{eq:BA}) in \Cref{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd2},
%
\[[A,B]x^{2m} = x^{2m+2} - x^{2m+1} \qquad\text{and}\qquad [A,B]x^{2m+1} = x^{2m+2} - x^{2m+3}\]
%
for all $m\in\nset_0$, i.e., $[A,B]\notin\fg$ by \Cref{thm:gCharac} (ii).
\exmsymbol
\end{exm}


In summary, for $A,B\in\fg$, in order to have
%
\[A+B\in\fg,\quad AB\in\fg,\quad BA\in\fg, \quad\text{and}\quad [A,B]\in\fg\]
%
it is sufficient that the operators $A$ and $B$ possess a common family $\{V_i\}_{i\in\nset_0}$ of invariant finite dimensional subspaces $V_i$ in \Cref{thm:gCharac} (iii).


\begin{cor}
Let $n\in\nset$ and let $\cV := \{V_i\}_{i\in\nset_0}$ be a family of subspaces $V_i\subseteq\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ such that
%
\[\dim V_i < \infty \qquad\text{and}\qquad \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]=\bigcup_{i\in\nset_0} V_i.\]
%
Then
%
\[\fg_\cV := \big\{A\in\fg \,\big|\, AV_i\subseteq V_i\ \text{for all}\ i\in\nset_0\big\}\]
%
is a regular Fr\'echet Lie algebra in $\fg$ with Lie bracket
%
\[[\,\cdot\,,\,\cdot\,]:\fg_\cV\times\fg_\cV\to\fg_\cV,\quad (A,B)\mapsto [A,B] := AB - BA.\]
%
The set $G_\cV := \exp(\fg_\cV)$ is the corresponding regular Fr\'echet Lie group.
\end{cor}


The question is, are all regular Fr\'echet Lie algebras in $\fg$ contained in some $\fg_\cV$?
For finite dimensional Lie algebras this is true.


\begin{cor}\label{cor:subspaceLieAlgebras}
Let $n\in\nset$ and let $\tilde{\fg}$ be a finite dimensional regular Fr\'echet Lie algebra in $\fg$.
Then there exists a family $\cV = \{V_i\}_{i\in\nset_0}$ of finite dimensional subspaces $V_i$ of $\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ with
%
\[\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n] = \bigcup_{i\in\nset_0} V_i\]
%
such that $\tilde{\fg}\subseteq\fg_\cV$.
\end{cor}
%
\begin{proof}
Let $g_1,\dots,g_N$ be a vector space basis of $\tilde{\fg}$, i.e., $\dim \tilde{\fg}=N\in\nset$.
Let
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:a1aN}
a_1,\dots,a_N\in\rset \quad\text{with}\quad a_1^2 + \dots + a_N^2 = 1.
\end{equation}
%
Then for any $\alpha\in\nset_0^n$ there exists a $D = D(\alpha)\in\nset$ such that
%
\[(a_1 g_1 + \dots + a_N g_N)^k x^\alpha \quad\subseteq\quad \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq D}\]
%
for all $a_1,\dots,a_N$ with (\ref{eq:a1aN}), since $(a_1 g_1 + \dots + a_N g_N)^k$ is  continuous in $a_1,\dots,a_N$ and the set (\ref{eq:a1aN}) is compact.
From here, continue as in the proof of \Cref{thm:gCharac} step ``[(i) $\Leftrightarrow$ (ii)] $\Rightarrow$ (iii)''.
\end{proof}


An infinite dimensional regular Fr\'echet Lie algebra of $\fg$ does not need to be contained in some $\fg_\cV$ as the following example shows.


\begin{exm}\label{exm:nonSubspaceLieAlgebra}
Let $n\in\nset$, let $y\in\rset^n$, and let
%
\[l_y: \rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset,\qquad f\mapsto l_y(f) := f(y).\]
%
Define
%
\[\fg_y := \big\{l_y\cdot p \,\big|\, p\in\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\big\}.\]
%
Then
%
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item $\fg_y\subseteq\fg$,
\item $\alpha A+\beta B\in\fg_y$ for all $A,B\in\fg_y$ and $\alpha,\beta\in\rset$,
\item $AB\in\fg_y$ for all $A,B\in\fg_y$,
\item $[A,B]\in\fg_y$ for all $A,B\in\fg_y$, and
\item $\fg_y$ is closed,
\end{enumerate}
%
i.e., $\fg_y$ is a regular Fr\'echet Lie algebra in $\fg$, since
%
\[\alpha A+\beta B=l_y\cdot(\alpha p+\beta q)\qquad\text{and}\qquad AB=q(y)\cdot l_y\cdot p\]
%
with $Af = f(y)\cdot p$, $Bf = f(y)\cdot q$, and $\alpha,\beta\in\rset$.
To see that $\fg_y$ is closed let
%
\[A_k = l_y\cdot p_k \qquad\text{with}\qquad A_k\xrightarrow{k\to\infty} \big[A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\big].\]
%
Hence, $A_k1 = p_k\to p\in\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ as $k\to\infty$ and $A=l_y\cdot p\in\fg_y$.
But since $\deg p$ in $A = l_y\cdot p$ is not bounded, $\fg_y$ is not contained in any $\fg_\cV$.
\exmsymbol
\end{exm}


In \Cref{dfn:regFLieGroup} we gave the definition of a regular Fr\'echet Lie group and its Lie algebra.
The Examples \ref{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd}, \ref{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd2}, and \ref{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd3} show that $\fg$ in the \Cref{dfn:fg} is not a regular Fr\'echet Lie algebra of any regular Fr\'echet Lie group $G$.
It is too large and not even a vector space.
However, since it contains all operators $A$ such that $e^{tA}$ is well-defined, it contains all regular Fr\'echet Lie algebras in our case.


The set $\fg$ and its regular Fr\'echet Lie algebras are therefore not only of practical interest in the Theorems \ref{thm:main}, \ref{thm:main2}, and \ref{thm:main4}, and \Cref{cor:makingSoS}, but they can also serve as simple toy examples to further study regular Fr\'echet Lie groups and their Lie algebras.



\section{Three-Line-Proof of Stochel's Theorem}%%%
\label{sec:stochel}%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


Recall from \Cref{sec:prelim}, for a real sequence $s = (s_\alpha)_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n}$ and a polynomial
%
\[p(x) = \sum_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n} p_\alpha\cdot x^\alpha \quad\in\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n],\]
%
the Riesz functional $L_s:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset$ is defined by
%
\[L_s(p) = \sum_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n} p_\alpha\cdot s_\alpha.\]
%
We get the following three-line-proof of Stochels Theorem \cite{stochel01}.


\begin{thm}
Let $n\in\nset$ and $K\subseteq\rset^n$ closed.
The following are equivalent:
%
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item $s$ is a $K$-moment sequence.

\item For all $d\in\nset_0$, $s|_{\leq d} := (s_\alpha)_{\alpha\in\nset_0^n:|\alpha|\leq d}$ is a truncated $K$-moment sequence.
\end{enumerate}
\end{thm}
%
\begin{proof}
(i) $\Rightarrow$ (ii): Clear.

(ii) $\Rightarrow$ (i): For $p\in\pos(K)$, $L_s(p) \overset{\deg p \leq d}{=} L_{s|_{\leq d}}(p) \overset{\text{(ii)}}{\geq} 0$ and, by Haviland's Theorem \cite{havila36}, $s$ is a $K$-moment sequence.
\end{proof}


Note, in (ii) actually a weaker property is used:
%
\begin{enumerate}[(ii')]
\item For all $d\in\nset_0$ and $p\in\pos(K)_{\leq d}$, $L_{s|_\leq d}(p)\geq 0$.
\end{enumerate}
%
The truncated sequences $s|_{\leq d}$ do not need to be truncated $K$-moment sequences.


\begin{rem}
