\section{Conclusions and Open Problem}%%%
\label{sec:open}%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


In \cite{didio24posPresConst,didio24posPres2arxiv,didio25hadamardLangerArxiv} we used the theory of regular Fr\'echet Lie groups and algebras to investigate linear maps
%
\[T:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\]
%
and
%
\[e^{tA}:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n].\]
%
We extended previous results \cite{guterman08,borcea11,netzer10} about positivity preserver.
Especially in \cite{didio24posPres2arxiv} we claimed that the maps $e^{tA}$ could be used in linear optimization.
This is shown in the current work by the Theorems \ref{thm:main} and \ref{thm:main2}.


In \Cref{cor:makingSoS} we give for every $d\in\nset_0$ and subset $S\subseteq\pos(K)_{\leq 2d}$ an explicit linear operator
%
\[A:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\to\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\]
%
such that $e^{tA} S$ is a subset of the sums of squares for all $t\geq\tau\geq 0$.
In the optimization
%
\[\min_{p\in S} L(p)\]
%
over a linear functional $L:\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}\to\rset$, using the Schmüdgen or the Putinar Positiv\-stellen\-satz can lead to degree blow ups in the $\sigma_e$ in (\ref{eq:schmDarst}) and (\ref{eq:putDarst}), even though the original problem lives only in $\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d}$.
This is circumvented by \Cref{cor:makingSoS} (iii), since the Schmüdgen and the Putinar Positiv\-stellen\-satz are removed.


But \Cref{thm:main} and \Cref{cor:makingSoS} depend on the fixed degree $d\in\nset_0$.
In \ref{thm:main2} we show that there exists an operator $A$ such that $e^A\pos(\rset^n)\subseteq\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2$.
But in \Cref{thm:main4} we see that, for compact $K\subseteq\rset^n$, there is not linear operator $A$ such that $e^A\pos(A)\subseteq\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2$.
In fact, there is no bijective linear operator $T$ with $T\pos(K)\subseteq\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2$ for compact $K$.


An open problem of the current work is the following.


\begin{open}\label{open2}
Exists a (good or useful) description of the sets
%
\[e^{\tau_{d_i} A}\big(S\cap\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d_i}\big) \qquad\text{or}\qquad e^{A}S\]
%
in $\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]_{\leq 2d_i}^2$ and $\sum\rset[x_1,\dots,x_n]^2$, respectively?
\end{open}


In \Cref{sec:fg} we look at the structure of the set $\fg$ of all linear maps $A$ such that $e^{tA}$ is well-defined.
We characterized all such maps $A$ in $\fg$.
We showed with explicit examples that $\fg$ is not a vector space (\Cref{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd}), $\fg$ is not closed under multiplication (\Cref{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd2}), and $\fg$ is not closed under the Lie bracket (\Cref{exm:ABshiftEvenOdd3}).
We looked at special regular Fr\'echet Lie algebras in $\fg$ in \Cref{cor:subspaceLieAlgebras} and \Cref{exm:nonSubspaceLieAlgebra}.
We have to work here in the full setting of non-commutative regular Fr\'echet Lie groups and algebras, where the theory is much more evolved than in the commutative case in \cite{didio24posPresConst}.


With respect to \Cref{open2} one also has to mention, that $\pos(K)$ or general subsets $S\subseteq\pos(K)$ are not spectrahedra or spectrahedral shadows \cite{netzer23GeometryOfLinearMatrixInequ}.
This property is preserved under linear maps like $e^{tA}$, i.e., an NP-hard optimization problem remains NP-hard and ``only'' the degree blow ups in the Schmüdgen and the Putinar Positiv\-stellen\-satz (\ref{eq:schmDarst}) and (\ref{eq:putDarst}) are removed.



