\section{Introduction}
The spontaneous emergence of spatially modulated phases constitutes a central topic in the study of complex many-body systems, encompassing a wide range of physical contexts. In classical systems, such modulated structures include, among others, cluster crystals~\cite{Prestipino_2014, Likos2007,Diaz2015,Mello_2023}, stripe phases~\cite{Christopher2000,Berg_2007,mendoza2012,Xiuzhen2012,Barci_2013,Mendoza_2015,Nicolao_2016,Mendoza_2020_2}, bubble configurations~\cite{Glaser2007,Saratz2010,mendoza2016,mendoza2019,Mendoza_2020_2}, and quasicrystals~\cite{Barkan2014,Dotera2014,Zu2017}, each resulting from the presence of some form of frustration in the system. These pattern formation phenomena have been extensively studied in soft condensed matter, vortex matter~\cite{Meng2014,Meng_2017,Zhao2017}, and colloidal assemblies~\cite{Mdalek2008,Lindquist2017,Zhuang2017}, where self-organization emerges from free energy minimization under specific pair interactions between particles. In quantum systems, the interplay between interactions and quantum fluctuations gives rise to even more exotic phases, including supersolids~\cite{Chomaz2019,Bottcher2019,Norcia2021,Ripley2023,Lima2025}, quantum cluster crystals~\cite{Cinti2014,Cinti_2014_1,Cinti2019,Mendoza_2021_2}, and quantum quasicrystals~\cite{Pupillo2020,Mendoza_2022,Zampronio_2024,Gross_2024,Mendoza2025,gross2025}, where superfluidity may coexist with non-trivial spatial order.


Among the class of interactions that drive self-assembly, ultra-soft repulsive potentials, characterized by a bounded core and a negative minimum in their Fourier transform, are responsible for the formation of cluster-crystalline states with particles that might overlap~\cite{Likos2007,Neuhaus_2011,Mendoza_2021,Mello_2023}. Typically these potentials produce the stabilization of cluster phases with an integer $n$ occupation number and isostructural transitions as the density is increased. Notably, these are first order phase transitions, that occur through the development of coexistence regions of phases with $n$ and $n+1$ occupancy numbers. Hence, at the beginning of the coexistence region, droplets of the $n+1$ cluster phase nucleates and, as the density increases, these droplets grow until the system presents a pure $n+1$ cluster phase and the coexistence region ends.

However, when a hard-core repulsion or additional sharp length scale is introduced at the short distances, the tendency to form overlapping clusters can be significantly inhibited, paving the way for the formation of new modulated states {with properties between solid and liquid phases, for which are sometimes called {\it mesophases}}, and that includes stripe phases, hexagonal lattices of bubbles or holes, dimer phases as well as quasicrystalline textures~\cite{Imperio2004, Imperio2006,Glaser2007,Dotera2014,Caprini2018,Fomin2021,Mambretti2021,Nogueira2022,Pini2024}.
The mechanism for the pattern formation stems from the competition between the ultra-soft component, which favors clustering, and the hard-core component, which penalizes such configurations~\cite{Caprini2018, Mambretti2021}. 
This combination of soft and hard-core repulsions is sometimes referred as {\it core-softened} potentials as well as {\it hard-core/soft-shoulder} or HCSS potentials \cite{Glaser2007}.
As a result, systems may settle into phases where the stabilized structure arises from the balance of competing interactions rather than simple clustering — a behavior observed in both classical and quantum contexts~\cite{Abreu2022}. 

In situations when the effective hard-core size is much lower than the spacing of the lattice formed by the arrangement of the clusters themselves, the particles belonging to a cluster develop an internal structure that minimizes the hard-core repulsion energy - typically a BCC or triangular lattice in three or two dimensions, respectively. Meanwhile, in this case, the long-distance physical properties of the cluster lattice remains essentially unaltered in comparison to the corresponding soft-core particle system~\cite{Caprini2018}. This kind of behavior naturally disappears as soon as the size of the clusters becomes comparable to the distance between them. In such regime the intra and inter cluster interactions are not well separated by an energy scale and, consequently, the behavior of the system becomes much richer. This scenario is exactly the one explored in Refs. \cite{Mambretti2021} and \cite{Pini2024}, where the authors investigated the properties of a self-assembled crystalline dimer phase in a regime where the hard-core size of the particles is comparable to the distance between dimers forming the lattice. Interestingly, the authors shows that the classical ground state is given by a ``nematic'' state in which dimers are oriented in a non-trivial direction with respect to the dimers lattice.  

Within this context, our work aims to present a systematic study of the self-assembling process in a 2D soft-core cluster forming model frustrated by a hard-core interaction. In order to do so, we consider a two dimensional GEM-4 model combined with an inverse power-law potential, as an effective hard-core interaction, and compute the exact classical ground state phase diagram, using as running parameters the density and the relative intensity between the GEM-4 interaction and the short-range power-law repulsion. Our results shows that the soft-core scenario, when the transition between clusters with increasing occupancy occurs as the density is increased, is significantly modified even when the size of the hard-core is relatively small. To be specific, the maximum cluster occupancy attained decreases rapidly with the size of the hard-core while a plethora of new single particle phases, not present in the phase diagram of the GEM-4 model, are developed. Finally, we would like to remark that, differently from previously studied scenarios in which the interparticle distance is much smaller than the distance between clusters, in our case the orientational order of the clusters is a non-trivial feature of the self assembling process. This makes the orientational order of the cluster states to vary along the phase diagram. 

The paper is organized as follows, in Sect.~\ref{model} we introduce the model studied and the method employed for the determination of the classical ground state of the system. The Sect.~\ref{results} shows the results obtained for the phase diagram of the system, initially implementing the direct energy minimization for pure phases only and, in a second moment, including the possibility of coexistence between different phases. Moreover, a detailed description of the phases stabilized by the system is presented making evident the rich physical behavior of the system studied. Finally, Sect.~\ref{conclusions} is devoted to present our final remarks and conclusions.

