This set of 11 different specific ansatz, represented in Figure \ref{fig:specific_ansatz}, constitutes the basis for constructing the zero-temperature phase diagram of the system. Each was used to evaluate the energy as:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:specific_ansatz}
  \frac{E}{N} = \frac{1}{2 n}\left(\sum_{l,m}^\infty\sum_{q,q'}^n V(|\textbf{R}_{lm}+\textbf{u}_{q}-\textbf{u}_{q'}|)\right),
\end{equation}
where $\textbf{R}_{lm}\equiv l\textbf{a}+m\textbf{b}$ and $\textbf{u}_{q+1}=\{d\cos(\phi+q\pi/3), d\sin(\phi+q\pi/3)\}$, for $q\geq 0$ and $\textbf{u}_0=\{0, 0\}$. Here the index convention is the same as in Eq.(\ref{eq:generic_ansatz}). Note that in general, especially for larger clusters, it may be necessary to modify the $\textbf{u}_q$, however, up to cluster 4, our parametrization will accurately describe cluster geometries.

The only exception to the description given by eq. (\ref{eq:specific_ansatz}) is the Cluster 3a ansatz. In this case clusters alternate orientation along the $\textbf{b}$ direction introducing a secondary sub-lattice in that direction. Nevertheless, in this case it is simple to realize that the total interaction energy of a trimer in both sub-lattices are equal. This results is straightforward if we observe that the trimers in both sub-latices are equivalent by a lattice reflection using $\textbf{a}$ as symmetry axis. For this reason, to evaluate the total energy we simply consider the value obtained for a single type of trimer.  

%-- the other being $C$, the relative strength between repulsive interactions in eq.~\ref{pot}
Furthermore, we should notice that at a given density $\rho$ the lattice parameters are not independent. In general, the density of particles can be evaluated as
\begin{equation}
    \rho = {n_{c}}/{A}, 
\end{equation}
where $n_{c}$ is the number of particles inside the basic cell of the pattern having an area $A$. For example, for the oblique lattice the basic cell area is given as $A=a b \sin\theta$, which lead us to the conclusion: 
\begin{equation}
b=\frac{n_{c}}{a \rho \sin\theta}.
\end{equation}

Taking into account this kind of constraint, we have as independent variational parameters $a$, $\theta$ and $d$ for all cluster phases, $a$ and $\theta$ for the oblique phase and just $a$ for the rectangular phase. On the other hand, the triangular, square, honeycomb and kagome phases are fully determined by the density, without any minimization. In order to enforce each ansatz to represent a single type of configuration we should impose additional constraints to the variational parameters as presented in~\ref{fig:specific_ansatz}.   

\section{Results}
\label{results}

\subsection{Phase diagram}
