\subsection{PAOA-Discovered Schedule Separation in Lévy SK}
\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{Supp5.pdf}
    \caption{The optimized two schedules for SK model with Lévy bonds using PAOA with $2p$ parameters averaged across 50 instances, where the initial schedule (green curve), which is $\exp(\text{linspace}(\log(0.005),\log(0.12),p=17))$, is used for both heavy and light nodes. The number of independent experiment used in the training is $10^5$ experiments. }
    \label{fig:SK_levy_PAOA}
\end{figure}
The results presented in section~\ref{sec:SK_model_with_Levy_bonds} are based on a grid search that identifies the optimal single and multi annealing schedules. Building on a single annealing schedule baseline, PAOA introduces a heterogeneous scheduling strategy by partitioning the graph into two groups: one with large coupling strengths (“heavy” nodes) and another with significantly smaller couplings (“light” nodes). Both groups initially share the same optimized schedule. As shown in FIG.~\ref{fig:SK_levy_PAOA}, PAOA adaptively modifies this schedule, accelerating the annealing of light nodes while maintaining a slower rate for heavy ones. Despite starting from an optimal schedule, PAOA’s learned adjustment introduces structured heterogeneity, which leads to improved performance, with the average energy per spin moving from $\langle E \rangle/n = -839.2348$ to $\langle E \rangle/n = -839.3907$. 





