\section{Optimization}
In addition to the Wineland parameter, we wish to control the magnitude of the mean magnetization and its direction $\vec n=\vec z$, as well as the squeezing axis in the final state $\ket{\psi_T}$. In order to do so we define the cost function to be minimized as:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{C} = \xi_W^2 + a_4\left(\langle J_{y} \rangle^2 + \langle J_{x} \rangle^2\right) + \mathcal{G}.
    \label{eq:C}
\end{equation}
In the equation above the second term is added to minimize the projection of the magnetization in the plane orthogonal to the target direction $z$. $\mathcal{G}$ is a function added to control the smoothness and duration of the pulse sequence and is discussed in Appendix~\ref{app:opt}.\\

For a given pulse sequence, specified by $3M$ parameters, the final state $\ket{\psi_T}=U\ket{\psi_0}$ is computed numerically using Eq.~\eqref{eq:U1}. Taking advantage of the translation invariance of the Hamiltonian and of the initial state one can restrict to the zero-momentum sector and reduce the size of the matrices by a factor $\mathcal{O}(N)$. The matrix exponentials are then computed using Padé approximation, as implemented in the JAX library~\cite{jax2018github}.\\

The optimization is carried out using gradient descent method and automatic differentiation. The details about the optimization algorithm, constraints and the values of the hyperparameters are discussed in Appendix~\ref{app:opt}.\\



