Optimizing the final state of a many-body system governed by a time-dependent Hamiltonian poses significant numerical challenges, particularly as the system size \( N \) increases—due to the exponential growth of the Hilbert space—or when the number of discrete time steps \( M \) becomes large. We first focus on relatively small spin chains with \( N \leq 10 \) and a moderate number of time steps, \( M = 30 \).\footnote{Some results for $M=100$ and $M=3$ are discussed in Sec.~\ref{sec:M_3_and_100}.} 
Fig.~\ref{fig:summary} shows the Wineland spin-squeezing parameter achieved by these optimized pulses across different system sizes. For systems up to \( N = 8 \) the resulting spin squeezing approaches the absolute lower bound given by $\xi_W^2= 1/(1 +N/2)$~\cite{Pezz__2018}.\footnote{This bound can be saturated by some specific linear combination of three Dicke states~\cite{Pezz__2018}.}
Notably, the squeezing obtained using optimized pulses significantly exceeds that obtained using a simple quench (time-independent Hamiltonian) with Van der Waals or dipolar interactions in ring geometries of comparable size (see the green and pink curves in Fig.~\ref{fig:summary}).\\

The control landscape is highly non-convex, featuring numerous local minima which complicate the optimization.
On the other hand, the tendency of different random initial conditions to converge to distinct local optima allows to sample a broad region of the state space. In particular, the states obtained by this method cover a wide range of mean magnetization.\footnote{It is also possible to add a term in the cost function to target a specific value of the mean magnetization $\langle J_z\rangle$ in the final state.}
Figure ~\ref{fig:histo8} presents the Wineland parameter and the mean magnetization for various output states obtained with \( N = 8 \). The entanglement depth $k$ is assessed using the criterion established in~\cite{Sorensen_2001}, which uses the value of $\xi_W^2$ to characterize multipartite entanglement. Specifically, if a data point lies in a region labelled by \( k > p \), the associated quantum state exhibits at least some \( p \)-partite entanglement. Based on this criterion, we observe that nearly all solutions exhibit entanglement depth greater than six. Moreover, states with a low Wineland parameter approaching the theoretical lower bound \( 2/(2+N) \), i.e., \( \xi_W^2 \lesssim 0.23 \) have a fidelity $F$ with a GHZ state above 0.5 and therefore possess some 8-partite entanglement (see Appendix~\ref{sec:ghz}).

In Figure~\ref{fig:histo8}, we highlight three particular SSS: the one exhibiting the highest degree of squeezing (red square), a state with both high magnetization and  8-partite entanglement (green diamond), and the state with the highest magnetization among all the states produced (blue cross). The panels a) to d) of Figure ~\ref{fig:8dynamics_basic} illustrate the time evolutions leading to the the first and third of these final states (highest squeezing and largest magnetization).
The panel a) of Figure \ref{fig:8dynamics_basic} shows the time evolution of the magnetization, obtained
by determining at each time step which direction $\vec n$ maximizes $|\langle J_{\vec n}\rangle|$.
The panel~b) displays the time evolution of the minimal variance. It is obtained by determining at each time step which direction $\vec m$ orthogonal to $\vec n$ minimizes $(\Delta J_{\vec{m}})^2$. The red  (resp. blue) curves correspond to the dynamics leading to the final state marked in red (resp. blue) in Fig.~\ref{fig:histo8}.
Note that due to the choice of the cost function Eq.~\eqref{eq:C}, the mean magnetization in the final state is along the $z$ axis and the variance is minimal in the $y$ direction.
During a large part of the time evolution, the variance -- which is directly related to  the correlation between the spins\footnote{The variance of the total magnetization is simply a combination of the connected two-point correlations $(\Delta J_{\vec{m}})^2=\sum_{ij} \langle \sigma_i^{\vec{m}} \sigma_j^{\vec{m}}\rangle^c$ with $\langle \sigma_i^{\vec{m}} \sigma_j^{\vec{m}}\rangle^c=\langle \sigma_i^{\vec{m}} \sigma_j^{\vec{m}}\rangle-\langle \sigma_i^{vec{m}}\rangle\langle \sigma_j^{vec{m}}\rangle$.} -- fluctuates in some highly nontrivial manner. It is interesting to notice that the variance drops at the end of the protocol. This drop is largely responsible for the low final value of $\xi_W^2$. Such a drop is observed in many cases and it is very pronounced in the case of the pulse leading to the most squeezed state (red curve). The time dependence of the magnetization, displayed in panel b) of Fig.~\ref{fig:histo8}, is no less complex, with some non monotonous behavior. 
The associated pulse sequenced are displayed in panels e) and f).
Although some parameters favoring some smooth time dependence  of the control fields are present in the cost function (see Appendix~\ref{app:opt}), the optimization process leads to pulses which are rather complex, without any obvious simple pattern. 
It is possible to enforce smoother pulses by increasing the parameters $a_1$ and $a_2$ in the cost function (Eq.~\ref{eq:G} in Appendix) but we observed that this rapidly deteriorates the value of $\xi_W^2$ in the final state. This indicates that the optimized pulse sequences exploit in a subtle way the interplay between the interaction part of the Hamiltonian and the external control fields along the $z$ and $x$ axis, and that some degree of complexity in the time dependence of the control pulses is important to approach highly squeezed states.

To further illustrate the structure of the final states, their Husimi-Q distributions \cite{husimi_formal_1940,Ma_2011} is displayed in the panels e) and f) of Fig.~\ref{fig:8dynamics_basic}. 
The Husimi-Q distribution is defined by $f(\vec n)=|\langle \vec n|\psi\rangle|^2$
where $|\vec n\rangle$ is a fully polarized product state with magnetization pointing in the $\vec n$ direction and $|\psi\rangle$ is the final state reached at time $t_{\rm final}$. This function allows to represents graphically  the fluctuations of the direction of the magnetization and to "visualize" the associated squeezing. In the case of the state with the strongest squeezing ($\xi_W^2=0.205$) the mean magnetization per site is relatively small ($2\langle J_z\rangle/N=0.092$) and we observe an almost complete delocalization of the magnetization direction along an annulus centered around the $y=0$ plane (blue region in panel (e) of Fig.~\ref{fig:8dynamics_basic}). The squeezing is less extreme for the state with $\xi_W^2=0.335$ (panel (f)), which has a mean magnetization that is much larger ($2\langle J_z\rangle/N=0.770$).

