Dephasing is an important source of noise in Rydberg atom simulators~\cite{leseleuc_analysis_2018,wurtz_aquila_2023}, and we evaluate here the robustness of the state preparation protocol under the effect of such source of decoherence. Using different pulses (those highlighted in Fig.~\ref{fig:histo8}),\footnote{Here dephasing is added on top of pulses which were optimized {\em without} decoherence. We leave for a future study the 
problem of the squeezing optimization in presence of dephasing.} using the library DynamiQs ~\cite{guilmin2025dynamiqs}, we simulate the Lindblad master equation
\begin{equation}
\partial_t \rho = -i[H,\rho] +\gamma\sum_{m =1}^L \left(\sigma^z_m\rho \sigma^z_m - \rho\right)
\label{eq:lindblad}
\end{equation} where $\gamma$ is the dephasing rate.

Figure~\ref{fig:8dephasing} presents the value of Wineland parameter at the final time as a function of $\gamma$. As expected, the Wineland parameter is reduced by dephasing. In addition we observe that the squeezing is more robust when when the final state has a large magnetization (blue curve) compared to a state with maximal squeezing but lower magnetization (red curve). An intermediate behavior is observed in cases where the squeezing is not optimal but still supports 8-partite entanglement (green curve). Notably, the simple quench protocol is also quite resilient to decoherence (pink curve). But whatever the value of $\gamma$ the  states produce by the time-independent Hamiltonian (quench) remain less squeezed than those obtained with the protocol that produces the SSS with the largest magnetization.

