The computing time required to calculate numerically the final state scales as \( \mathcal{O}\left(M (2^N/N)^3\right) \), and optimizing control pulses for large systems is therefore very challenging. So, to generate 
squeezed states on larger systems we attempt to repurpose the optimized pulse sequences obtained for small $N$ in larger systems.
We refer to “scalability” as the capacity of a pulse sequence to generate squeezed states in systems exceeding the size of the system on which it was initially optimized.
It turns out that most pulses constructed for $N=8$ fail to produce 
high squeezing in larger systems.\footnote{For instance, the lowest Wineland parameter achieved for $N=12$ with this method is \( \xi_W^2 \approx 0.29 \), whereas the same pulse applied to a \( N = 8 \) system yields \( \xi_W^2 \approx 0.25 \) (shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:histo8}).} But, as we show below, some pulses optimized for \( N = 10 \) turn out be more scalable. 
Figure~\ref{fig:histo10} presents the Wineland parameter and mean magnetization of  various states obtained from different random initial pulses for \( N = 10 \). Using the same criteria used previously to characterize multipartite entanglement, we see that a significant number of states display an entanglement depth greater than 8. The highest spin squeezing (state marked with a red square) achieved is \( \xi_W^2 = 0.203 \). Compared to the absolute lower bound ($\xi_W^2 = 1/6$ for $N=10$), this value is not as good as what could be obtained for $N=8$.  But the main advantage of the  pulses  obtained for $N=10$ is that some of them can produce some low value of $\xi_W^2$ on larger systems. In other words, they are scalable.

