For any $x \in \mathcal{X}$, denote
\begin{alignat*}{3}
    \mu_t(x) &= E[Y(t)|X =  x], 
    &\mu_t &= E[\mu_t(X)], & \\
    e_t(x) &= P(T =t|X=x), 
    &e_t &= E[e_t(X)], & \\
    e_{ta}(x) &= P(T=t|X=x,T\in\ta),
    &\quad e_{ta} &= E[e_{ta}(X)|T\in\ta], \\
    \tau_{t,t'}(x) &= \mu_{t}(x) - \mu_{t'}(x),
    &\quad \tau_{t,t'} &= \mu_{t} - \mu_{t'}.
\end{alignat*}
For any generic function (possibly depending on $t$ and $a$), we write $g_t(\mathcal{X}_g) = E[g_t(X)|X\in \mathcal{X}_g]$ and $g_{ta}(\mathcal{X}_g) = E[g_{ta}(X)|X\in \mathcal{X}_g, T \in \mathcal{T}_a]$, e.g.~$\mu_t(\mathcal{X}_g) = E[\mu_t(X)|X\in \mathcal{X}_g]$.
The main goal in the following is to understand the causal content of two statistical estimands defined for known $a,a'$ and $g,g'$:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Group differences-in-means (DiM) 
    \begin{align}
    & E[Y| T \in \mathcal{T}_a, X \in \mathcal{X}_g] - E[Y| T \in \mathcal{T}_{a'}, X \in \mathcal{X}_g]  \notag \\
     - \Big( &E[Y| T \in \mathcal{T}_a, X \in \mathcal{X}_{g'}] - E[Y| T \in \mathcal{T}_{a'}, X \in \mathcal{X}_{g'}] \Big). \label{eq_DiM}
\end{align}
    \item Adjusted group differences-in-means (adjusted DiM) 
    \begin{align}
    & E[E[Y| T \in \mathcal{T}_a, X] | X \in \mathcal{X}_g] - E[E[Y| T \in \mathcal{T}_{a'}, X] | X \in \mathcal{X}_g] \notag  \\
     - \Big( &E[E[Y| T \in \mathcal{T}_a, X] | X \in \mathcal{X}_{g'}] - E[E[Y| T \in \mathcal{T}_{a'}, X] | X \in \mathcal{X}_{g'}] \Big). \label{eq_adjDiM}
\end{align}
\end{itemize}
The DiM contrasts differences of mean outcomes between treatment aggregations $a$ and $a'$ of groups $g$ and $g'$. It is typically analyzed in randomized controlled trials (RCTs) where access to aggregated treatments is subject to complete randomization or stratified randomization at the group level. 
It boils down to interaction coefficient \eqref{eq:toy-beta3} in the motivating example of Section \ref{sec:toy-exmpl}.  The adjusted DiM, on the other hand, additionally balances the distribution of confounding factors relevant for observational studies.\footnote{In principle, the adjusted DiM could be based on confounding variables that only assure unconfoundedness on the level of $\ta$ and $\tap$ respectively. These do not, in general, have to be identical to the $X$ used in Assumption \ref{ass_CIA}. In fully observational studies, it is usually hard to conceive different confounding variables between effective and aggregated treatments. Our approach can be extended at the expense of more involved derivations and additional selection bias components, see Appendix \ref{app:deriv-acm-xtilde} for more details.} This is the population quantity behind contrasting aggregated matching estimands between groups or, equivalently, projecting signals for conditional effects onto group indicators \cite{Semenova2021DebiasedFunctions}.
In the case of homogeneous treatments and (conditional) independence, both estimands identify the difference of the canonical conditional/group average treatment effects. 

In the following, we separate sources of heterogeneity to clarify the interpretation of these common statistical estimands in terms of their causal content. For compactness, the decompositions are first introduced for one (adjusted) group mean in Section \ref{sec:decomp-single-mean} before combining them in Section \ref{sec:decomp-diffs} and Section \ref{sec:decomp-diff-in-diffs} to decompose any differences of interest. For simplicity, we treat larger outcomes as preferable throughout the discussion.

\paragraph{Remark 1.} We note that estimands \eqref{eq_DiM} and \eqref{eq_adjDiM} do not cover a variety of important research designs such as instrumental variables and differences-in-differences. We believe that many of the contributions of this paper can be extended to these settings. However, even for the arguably simpler estimands \eqref{eq_DiM} and \eqref{eq_adjDiM}, the double aggregation setting has not been studied and reveals non-trivial complexities. Thus, we leave the corresponding extensions to other designs for future research. 


\subsection{Decomposing a Single (Adjusted) Conditional Mean} \label{sec:decomp-single-mean}

\subsubsection{Separating a Synthetic Stratified Experiment} \label{sec:decomp1}

We now isolate the part that only depends on group heterogeneity from parts also depending on additional covariates (see Section \ref{sec:toy-exmpl2} how the latter may drive heterogeneity). Under Assumption \ref{ass_CIA}, the conditional mean can be decomposed as
\begin{align} \label{eq:decomp-cm1}
    E[Y|T\in\mathcal{T}_a,X\in \xg] %& = \sum_{t\in\ta} E[\mathbbm{1}(T=t)Y(t)|T\in\mathcal{T}_a,X\in \xg]  \nonumber \\
    & = \sum_{t\in\ta} 
    \underbrace{e_{ta}(\xg) \mu_t(\xg)}_{\text{Stratified RCT}}
  + \underbrace{\frac{Cov(e_t(X),\mu_t(X) | X\in \xg)}{P(T\in\mathcal{T}_a | X\in \xg)}}_{\text{Individualized targeting}} \nonumber \\
  & =: d(a,g) + d_{4}(a,g).
\end{align}

The first component $d(a,g)$ corresponds to a conditional mean that would be obtained in a hypothetical DGP with treatments assigned as in a group stratified RCT while leaving the potential outcome distributions untouched. The treatment probabilities in this synthetic RCT estimand correspond to the observed group probabilities $e_{ta}(\xg)$ and therefore vary only at the group-level. The second term $d_{4}(a,g)$ represents contributions stemming from variables beyond group membership. The numerator is the covariance of the actual treatment probabilities and the respective potential outcomes. It captures individualized targeting going beyond heterogeneity group specific targeting. It is positive (negative) if subgroups with higher potential outcomes for a particular treatment tend to have a higher (lower) probability of receiving it. The denominator normalizes by the probability to be observed in the particular treatment aggregation $a$. %and is constant over all $t\in\ta$. 

The adjusted conditional mean can be decomposed in a similar manner but takes into account the adjustment for covariates beyond the heterogeneity group:
\begin{align} \label{eq:decomp-cm2}
    &E[E[Y|T\in\mathcal{T}_a,X]|X\in \xg] \nonumber \\ %= \sum_{t\in\ta} E[E[ \mathbbm{1}(T=t)Y(t)|T\in\mathcal{T}_a,X] |X\in \xg]  \nonumber \\
    & = \sum_{t\in\ta} \underbrace{e_{ta}(\xg) \mu_t(\xg)}_{\text{Stratified RCT}} + \underbrace{Cov[e_{ta}(X), \mu_{t}(X) |X\in \xg]}_{\text{Conditional individualized targeting}}  \nonumber \\ &\quad + \underbrace{[E[e_{ta}(X) |X\in \xg] - e_{ta}(\xg)] \mu_{t}(\xg)}_{\text{Aggregate composition adjustment}}  \nonumber \\
    % & = \sum_{t\in\ta} \underbrace{e_{ta}(\xg) \mu_t(\xg)}_{\text{Stratified RCT}} + \underbrace{Cov[e_{ta}(X), \mu_{t}(X) |X\in \xg]}_{\text{Adjusted targeting}} + \underbrace{E[e_{ta}(X) |X\in \xg] \mu_{t}(\xg) - d_{\mathcal{T}_a}(\xg)}_{\text{Adjusted treatment composition}}  \nonumber \\
    & =: d(a,g) + d_{4'}(a,g) + d_{5}(a,g).
\end{align}
$d(a,g)$ is to the same synthetic stratified RCT as in \eqref{eq:decomp-cm1}. 
$d_{4'}(a,g)$ captures again whether treatment assignment is individually targeted. However, it uses the covariances with the propensity score conditional on being in aggregation $a$, $e_{ta}(X)$, instead of the unconditional $e_{t}(X)$. It therefore takes into account that the effective treatment probabilities \textit{within} the analyzed treatment $a$ may vary beyond the heterogeneity group $g$.\footnote{\label{fn:d4s} To see this observe how the denominators differ when rewriting the individualized targeting components as $d_{4}(\mathcal{T}_a,\xg) = Cov\left(\frac{e_t(X)}{P(T\in\mathcal{T}_a | X\in \xg)},\mu_t(X) | X\in \xg\right)$ and $d_{4'}(\mathcal{T}_a,\xg) = Cov\left(\frac{e_t(X)}{P(T\in\mathcal{T}_a | X)},\mu_t(X) | X\in \xg\right)$.}
$d_{5}(a,g)$ is a consequence of adjusting the confounder distribution in $a$ to match the population distribution. It contrasts two hypothetical stratified RCTs that leave the potential outcome distribution untouched but use different assignment probabilities. 
Both assignment probabilities are averages over the conditional probability of treatment $t$ within aggregation $a$. The first averages $e_{ta}(X)$ over the whole group $g$. The second averages $e_{ta}(X)$ over group $g$ within aggregate treatment $a$ only, i.e.~uses the actual group probabilities $e_{ta}(\xg) = E[e_{ta}(X)|X\in\xg,T\in\mathcal{T}_a]$. 
%The first one uses probabilities $E[e_{ta}(X) |X\in \xg]$ for assignment, i.e.~the conditional probability of treatment $t$ within aggregation $a$, $e_{ta}(X)$, averaged within subgroup $g$. The second one uses the actual group probabilities $e_{ta}(\xg) = E[e_{ta}(X)|X\in\xg,T\in\mathcal{T}_a]$. 
$d_{5}(a,g)$ is therefore driven by variation in propensity scores going beyond the group level and its interaction with treatment specific outcomes. %and has no clean targeting interpretation beyond that.


Equations \eqref{eq:decomp-cm1} and \eqref{eq:decomp-cm2} highlight that interpretation of seemingly simple statistical estimands becomes intricate once the analyzed treatment is composed of multiple effective treatments. However, both represent expected and familiar quantities in the special case where the analyzed treatment coincides with one effective treatment, i.e.~$\mathcal{T}_a = \{t\}$. \eqref{eq:decomp-cm1} then simplifies to the conditional average potential outcome under treatment $E[Y(t)|T=t, X\in \xg]$\footnote{To see this note that $Cov\left(\frac{e_t(X)}{e_t(\xg)},\mu_t(X) | X\in \xg\right) = E[Y(t)|T=t, X\in \xg] - \mu_t(\xg)$.}, and \eqref{eq:decomp-cm2} collapses to the conditional average potential outcome $\mu_t(\xg)$.\footnote{A similar result can be obtained under the treatment variation irrelevance assumption of \citeA{VanderWeele2009ConcerningInference} that at least the conditional average potential outcomes do not vary between effective treatments, i.e.~$\mu_t(x) = \mu_{t'}(x) ~\forall~x\in \mathcal{X},~ t, t' \in \mathcal{T}_a$. Many following results could also be obtained under suitable treatment variation irrelevance assumptions. However, we avoid formalizing them for compactness.}

Both decompositions are identical in two scenarios. First, if heterogeneity groups and confounders coincide like in an actually group stratified RCT as then $d_{4}(a,g) = d_{4'}(a,g) = d_{5}(a,g) = 0$. Second, if at least the analyzed treatment is (stratified) randomly assigned ensuring that $P(T\in\mathcal{T}_a | X) = P(T\in\mathcal{T}_a | X\in \xg)$ as then $d_{4}(a,g) = d_{4'}(a,g)$ (see footnote \ref{fn:d4s}) and $d_{5}(a,g) = 0$.\footnote{To see this note that then $E[e_{ta}(X)|X\in\xg] = e_{ta}(\xg) = \frac{e_t(\xg)}{P(T\in\mathcal{T}_a | X\in \xg)}$ for all $t\in\ta$.
    % \begin{align*}
    % for ~ all~t\in\ta & \\
    % e_{ta}(x) &:= P(T=t|X=x,T\in\mathcal{T}_a) = \frac{P(T=t|X=x)}{P(T\in\mathcal{T}_a|X=x)} \\
    % e_{ta}(\xg) &:= P(T=t|X\in\xg,T\in\mathcal{T}_a) = E[e_{ta}(X)|X\in\xg,T\in\mathcal{T}_a] \\
    % E[e_{ta}(X)|X\in\xg,T\in\mathcal{T}_a] & = E[\frac{ P(T=t|X=x)}{P(T\in\mathcal{T}_a|X=x)}|X\in\xg,T\in\mathcal{T}_a] \\
    % & = E[ \frac{P(T=t|X=x)}{P(T\in\mathcal{T}_a | X\in \xg)}|X\in\xg,T\in\mathcal{T}_a] \\
    % & = \frac{E[P(T=t|X=x)|X\in\xg,T\in\mathcal{T}_a]}{P(T\in\mathcal{T}_a | X\in \xg)} \\
