Figure \ref{fig:decomp} depicts the decomposition of the gender differences estimated by (a) DiM and (b) adjusted DiM. The left two panels decompose the group specific effect estimates and the right panels the respective difference. Both unadjusted and adjusted DiM are substantially larger for men. The decomposition reveals that these differences are almost entirely driven by women being targeted worse on average outcomes as captured by $\Delta_2$. In fact, women are targeted worse than random. To see this note that $\delta_0 + \delta_1$ represents the expected result under hypothetically fully randomized assignment of vocational training. Then men and women would benefit to the same extent as indicated by the close to zero $\Delta_1$. However, the substantially negative $\delta_2$ as well as the close to zero $\delta_3$ and $\delta_{4(')}$ reveals that training of women is targeted worse than this fully random benchmark, while it is better than random targeted for men due to a positive $\delta_2$. In principle, targeting could still be better than random if a negative $\delta_2$ is overcompensated by targeting well on group/individualized effects. This would be captured by positive $\delta_3$ and/or $\delta_{4(')}$. However, as they are close to zero, this is not the case for women in Job Corps. This indicates that there is room for improvement by differently targeting vocational training for women. Our results suggest that removing the worse than random targeting of vocational training for women could decrease the gender gap in the effectiveness of access to JC.


