\section{Mathematical Formulation}
\label{sec-MathematicalFormulation}

Let $(\Omega,\calF,\Q)$ be a complete probability space representing an arbitrage-free financial  market. %That is, $\Q$ is taken to be a risk-neutral probability measure with respect to both financial risk and mortality risk.\footnote{Refer to the discussion of \citet[Section 5.4]{Biffis-2005} and \citet[Section 3]{Shevchenko-2016} for further remarks on the use of risk-neutral valuation in the presence of both financial and mortality risk.} \footnote{Since \citet{Moenig-2021} adopts a utility maximization framework, the author's mathematical model is set in the historical probability space $(\Omega,\calF,\prob)$. However, \citet{Moenig-2021} also uses the subjective risk-neutral valuation framework of \citet{Moenig-2016}. In this case, the risk-neutral probability measure $\Q$ is taken to be the product measure of the risk-neutral measure for the financial market and the historical measure for individuals' mortality risk, which is consistent with actuarial pricing principles.} We assume that financial risk and mortality risk are independent.

