For the VA contract, we assume that withdrawals, fee charges, and other adjustments occur at discrete points in time. Let $\{t_0,t_1,\dots,t_N\}$ be a partition of the interval $[0,T]$, where $t_0=0$ is the inception of the contract and $t_N=T$ is the contract maturity. We assume that the partition is uniformly spaced, with $\Delta t = t_{k}-t_{k-1}$ for all $k=1,\dots,N$. In particular, the subset $\{t_1,\dots,t_N\}$ are the contractually specified times at which the policyholder can withdraw funds under the VA contract and at which the ratcheting mechanism is applied to adjust the benefit base. 

At each $t=t_k$, $k=1,\dots,N-1$, we assume the following sequence of events:
\begin{enumerate}
    % \item At the instant prior to any withdrawals, indicated by $t_k^-$, relevant fees, namely the investment and administrative fee $\varphi^{IA}$ and the guarantee fee $\varphi^G$, are charged proportionally against the VA investment account value $X(t_k^-)$. The fees $\varphi^{IA}$ and $\varphi^G$ are expressed in annual terms.

    \item At the instant prior to any withdrawals, indicated by $t_k^-$, the guarantee fee $\varphi$, is charged proportional to the VA investment account value $X(t_k^-)$. The fee $\varphi$ is expressed in annual terms and is assumed to cover the cost of financing the guarantee and any associated investment or administrative fees. % {\color{cyan} [Len: I removed the administrative fee $\varphi^{IA}$, since in the numerical implementation, it was difficult to determine the fair \textit{guarantee fee} in the presence of a nonzero administrative fee. In this case, we assume that the guarantee fee covers the cost of financing the guarantee and any associated investment or administrative fees.]}

