\section{Alternative benefit base Update Schemes}

In this paper, the guaranteed withdrawal amount $g(t_k)$ is calculated as a fixed proportion $\beta$ of the \textit{prevailing} benefit base $G(t_k^-)$ prior to withdrawals. Depending on the contract specification, $G(t)$ may increase over time (if there is a roll-up or a ratchet mechanism) or stays constant throughout the life of the contract, provided that no excess withdrawals are made. This is in contrast to return-of-premium GMWB riders \citep[see e.g.][]{Moenig-2016} where the guaranteed withdrawal amount is the initial premium $P_0$ divided across the withdrawal dates; if the policyholder withdraws the guaranteed amount at each withdrawal time, the total amount of withdrawals (ignoring the time value of money) is $P_0$. As such, the benefit base for return-of-premium GMWB riders is defined as the remaining guaranteed withdrawal amount, which is reduced periodically by the amount withdrawn by the policyholder and by any penalties associated to excess withdrawal beyond the guaranteed amount; see e.g. \citet[equations (2, 2', 2'')]{Moenig-2016}. %or \citet[equations (17)--(19)]{Shevchenko-2016}. 
In this specification, the prevailing benefit base \textit{indirectly} affects the withdrawal amount as a quantity that affects the maximum possible withdrawal \citep[see e.g.][following equation (6)]{Moenig-2016}.

In the main discussion, $g(t_k)$ is calculated after applying a ratchet mechanism between $G(t_k^-)$ and $X^{PF}(t_k^-)$; see equation \eqref{eqn-GuaranteedWithdrawalAmount}. To examine the effect of the ratchet mechanism on the fair guarantee fee and the policyholder's optimal withdrawal strategy, we alternatively consider a roll-up mechanism. Let $\delta \geq 0$ denote the per-annum roll-up rate. Under the roll-up mechanism, the guaranteed withdrawal amount is calculated as \[g(t_k) = \beta G(t_k^-)(1 + \delta \Delta t)\] and the benefit base is updated after the withdrawal as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item If $w(t_k) \leq g(t_k)$, then \[G(t_k^+) = \hat{G}(t_k^-) := G(t_k^-)(1 + \delta \Delta t).\]

    \item If $w(t_k) > g(t_k)$, then the benefit base is reduced by the same mechanism as in equation \eqref{eqn-Update-BenefitBase-Excess}. Note that in the event of an excess withdrawal, the roll-up is applied first, then the penalties are applied.
\end{itemize}
The case $\delta = 0$ corresponds to when the benefit base stays the same, except for when it decreases due to the penalties from excess withdrawal.

\begin{rem}
    The excess withdrawal deduction on the benefit base, given by equation \eqref{eqn-Update-BenefitBase-Excess}, leads to a reduction in future guaranteed withdrawals. In contrast, the excess penalty change $\zeta$ leads to a reduction in the current withdrawal amount. These two penalty schemes may be active at the same time in a single contract.
\end{rem}

\begin{rem}
    A modification of the benefit base update scheme necessitates changes to two functions in the current \texttt{R} codes:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item In the function \texttt{guar\_with()}, add two arguments \texttt{roll\_up\_rate} ($ = \delta$) and \texttt{update\_type} ( = ``ratchet'' or ``roll-up''). The value returned by the function is given by the following if-else statement.
\begin{verbatim}
if (update_type = ``roll-up'') {
    value <- beta * gamma * (1 + delta)
} else if (update_type = ``ratchet'') {
    x_postfee <- inv_acct_postfee(inv_acct_pre = x, fees = fees, delta_t = delta_t)
    value <- beta * pmax(x_postfee, gamma)
} else {
    print(``Update type is invalid'')
}
\end{verbatim}

        Since \texttt{guar\_with()} is used within \texttt{inv\_acct\_update()}, the new arguments must also be included in the latter function, with the call to \texttt{guar\_with()} updated with the additional arguments.

        \item In \texttt{guar\_base\_update()}, the two additional arguments must also be included, which are then passed to the call to \texttt{guar\_with()}. Following this step, the remainder of the function is as is, since the output of \texttt{guar\_with()} already reflects the benefit base update scheme; that is, equation \eqref{eqn-Update-PostWithdrawalGuaranteeBase} is the same, except that $\text{\texttt{guar\_with\_amt}} = \mathfrak{g}(x,\gamma) = \beta \gamma (1 + \delta)$ if the update scheme is a roll-up, and $\mathfrak{g}(x,\gamma) = \beta \max \{\mathfrak{f}(x), \gamma\}$ if the update scheme is a ratchet.

        % {\color{red} Double-check if the update mechanism itself must be updated, since \texttt{guar\_with\_amt} already reflects the roll-up or ratchet update for calculating the guaranteed withdrawal amount. That is, equation \eqref{eqn-Update-PostWithdrawalGuaranteeBase} is the same, except that $\text{\texttt{guar\_with\_amt}} = \mathfrak{g}(x,\gamma) = \beta \gamma (1 + \delta)$ if the update scheme is a roll-up, and $\mathfrak{g}(x,\gamma) = \beta \max \{\mathfrak{f}(x), \gamma\}$ if the update scheme is a ratchet.}
    \end{itemize}
\end{rem}

